 but modern scientific practice depends just as much on_newline_managing them: storing, comparing, reproducing, integrating with prior work, and reasoning about_newline_what they imply and what they fail to establish. In many fields this management has become a_newline_scaling bottleneck: the volume of reports, datasets, pipelines, and partial replications exceeds any_newline_human (or committee) capacity for reliable synthesis._newline_    This paper proposes a representation that is simultaneously ontological (recording the struc-_newline_ture of an experiment in a reusable schema), epistemic (recording what the experiment does to_newline_a belief system, in a way that tolerates conflict and supports incremental updating), and compu-_newline_tational (designed so that logic-based inference engines—including PLN-like systems—can exploit_newline_the structure to draw nontrivial inferences across experimental records, datasets, and semantically_newline_parsed text)._newline__newline_1.1   Why a logical representation rather than YAML?_newline_A well-designed configuration schema (YAML, JSON, etc.) can record experiment metadata and_newline_results effectively, and for many workflows it suffices. A purely syntactic schema, however, is_newline_not by itself a strong substrate for reasoning. The approach here is justified only insofar as the_newline_representation supports capabilities that are hard to obtain from a configuration format alone:_newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                      1_newline_    • Constraint-aware normalization. Two experiments can be automatically recognized as_newline_      “the same experiment up to irrelevant renaming” or “variants differing only in factor X,”_newline_      because factors, factor-levels, and targets are explicit first-class objects._newline__newline_    • Inference over validity and comparability. Threats to validity (leakage, confounding, in-_newline_      complete data, faulty comparisons) can be represented as objects that participate in inference_newline_      rules, so that downstream conclusions inherit appropriate caveats._newline__newline_    • Cross-source integration. Semantic parsing can turn prose claims into candidate proposi-_newline_      tions; dataset ingestion can turn tables into measurement objects; the system can link both_newline_      into a unified graph where inference bridges text, data, and experimental design._newline__newline_    • Epistemic aggregation under conflict. Multiple experiments may support and oppose_newline_      the same claim. The representation stores both without forcing premature collapse, en-_newline_      abling downstream AI components to reconcile, segment by context, or propose discriminating_newline_      follow-up experiments._newline__newline_    • Search by meaning, not by string. One can query for “all experiments estimating X_newline_      under regime Y with window size T and dictionary capacity D” rather than relying on_newline_      keyword matching._newline__newline__newline_2     EXPO: The Ontological Backbone_newline_EXPO (Ontology of Scientific Experiments), anchored to the SUMO upper ontology, is an interme-_newline_diate ontology layer designed to capture experiment concepts that are common across domains. It_newline_provides a reusable vocabulary in which goals, factors, target variables, actions, results, conclusions,_newline_and errors are explicit and comparable._newline_    EXPO plays two roles in our representation:_newline__newline_    1. Canonical structure. It supplies the stable “shape” of an experiment record—what parts_newline_       exist and how they relate._newline__newline_    2. Interoperability. It provides a shared semantic scaffold so that experiments in different_newline_       domains can still be aligned at the level of design and results._newline__newline_Modeling style. EXPO treats an experiment as an object with (at least) three conceptual levels:_newline_a physical/world level (the field of study), a model level (the experimental model or domain model),_newline_and a design level (parameters, target variables, actions). Experiments are assembled from a small_newline_set of core relations inherited from SUMO conventions—instance, subclass, part, attribute,_newline_and role—together with experiment-specific parts: ExperimentalDesign, ExperimentalModel,_newline_ExperimentalGoal, ExperimentalResults, ExperimentalConclusion, and so on. The design_newline_and model are elaborated through Factor, FactorLevel, TargetVariable, ModelAssumption,_newline_ResearchHypothesis, NullHypothesis, and AlternativeHypothesis._newline__newline__newline_3     Hyperseed Epistemics as an Overlay on EXPO_newline_EXPO records what an experiment is; our aim is also to make experiment records usable objects_newline_for automated reasoning—aggregating evidence across studies, representing and analyzing conflicts,_newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                   2_newline_connecting claims to parsed text and structured data, and proposing discriminating follow-up ex-_newline_periments. To this end we add an epistemic layer inspired by Hyperseed, a formal-logic-based_newline_ontology that aims to formalize a coherent conceptual account of the physical and mental worlds,_newline_and the knowledge and reasoning of cognitive agents therein._newline_    Hyperseed provides a compact, mathematically grounded treatment of epistemics that comple-_newline_ments EXPO’s structural descriptions. The central idea is to represent knowledge as an evidence_newline_state over a claim language rather than as a brittle set of Boolean assertions. In the simplest useful_newline_instance, evidence values live in V = [0, 1]2 : a “p-bit” recording separate degrees of positive and_newline_negative support. These assemble into evidence states E : L → V over a proposition language L._newline_    This representation is directly suited to experiment corpora, where evidence is routinely incom-_newline_plete, context-dependent, and sometimes contradictory across laboratories, datasets, and evaluation_newline_protocols. With p-bit evidence states, conflict does not force collapse: strong support and strong_newline_counter-support can coexist explicitly, allowing downstream reasoning to remain nontrivial._newline__newline_Division of labor. The two layers have a clean separation of concerns: EXPO provides the_newline_structural object—a record with explicit factors, target variables, actions, and measurement tables—_newline_while Hyperseed provides the belief-update interpretation: the experiment induces an evidence_newline_increment on a chosen claim language (e.g. performance, robustness, causal effect), and these_newline_increments can be aggregated across experiments._newline_    Concretely, for each derived claim c we attach a p-bit E(c) = (E + (c), E − (c)) ∈ [0, 1]2 , where_newline_E (c) summarizes positive support and E − (c) summarizes counter-support (including threats to_newline_  +_newline__newline_validity, suspected leakage, untested assumptions, or replication failures). This enables meta-_newline_reasoning operations such as aggregating evidence across experiments without erasing disagreement,_newline_propagating caveats through derived conclusions, identifying “controversial” claims with simulta-_newline_neously high E + and E − , and proposing follow-up experiments that most reduce uncertainty or_newline_resolve conflicts._newline__newline_Relation to PLN-style inference. PLN-style inference benefits from explicit structure: typed_newline_predicates, explicit factor levels, measurement semantics, and explicit uncertainty representations._newline_EXPO supplies the first three; Hyperseed supplies the fourth in a lightweight but mathematically_newline_disciplined form that supports principled aggregation and closure reasoning. In this sense Hyperseed_newline_is not a competing ontology but an epistemic semantics that makes EXPO experiment records more_newline_actionable for automated inference._newline__newline__newline_4     Design Requirements_newline_We want experiment records that satisfy:_newline_    1. Within-domain uniformity: similar experiments in a domain should map to the same_newline_       schema._newline_    2. Cross-domain compatibility: different domains share top-level experiment structure and_newline_       common measurement primitives._newline_    3. Reasoning affordances: the representation should support consistency checks (missing_newline_       components, incompatible units, contradictory claims), comparative queries (find experiments_newline_       with the same factor levels or similar results), downstream reasoning (derive conclusions from_newline_       result measurements), and epistemic updating (new experiments add support or counter-_newline_       support to existing claims)._newline__newline_                                                  3_newline_5     A Practical EXPO-to-MeTTa Mapping_newline_5.1   Core idea_newline_Each experiment is represented as a MeTTa object graph whose nodes are instances of EXPO_newline_(and domain-extension) classes and whose edges are SUMO/EXPO relations (instance, part,_newline_attribute, role, etc.)._newline__newline_5.2   Type signatures (informal)_newline_Even without a full dependent type system, structure can be enforced by conventions. The following_newline_classes form the core vocabulary:_newline__newline_; --- EXPO classes ---_newline_expo:ScientificExperiment_newline_expo:ComputationalExperiment_newline_expo:ExperimentalDesign_newline_expo:ExperimentalModel_newline_expo:ExperimentalGoal_newline_expo:ExperimentalResults_newline_expo:ExperimentalConclusion_newline_expo:Factor_newline_expo:FactorLevel_newline_expo:TargetVariable_newline_expo:ResearchHypothesis_newline_expo:ModelAssumption_newline__newline_; --- Extended measurement/epistemic classes ---_newline_exmeta:Dataset_newline_exmeta:MeasurementTable_newline_exmeta:MeasurementRow_newline_exmeta:Measurement_newline_exmeta:Claim_newline_exmeta:EvidenceState          ; Hyperseed-style epistemic object_newline__newline_5.3   Canonical relations_newline_; Membership_newline_(instance <individual> <class>)_newline__newline_; Composition / structure_newline_(part <part> <whole>)_newline__newline_; Attributes and roles_newline_(attribute <entity> <attribute>)_newline_(role <role-holder> <role-in-context>)_newline__newline_; Experiment-specific links_newline_(hasFactor        <experiment-or-model> <factor>)_newline__newline__newline_                                                4_newline_(hasFactorLevel   <factor> <factorLevel>)_newline_(hasTargetVariable <experiment-or-model> <targetVariable>)_newline_(hasAssumption    <experiment-or-model> <assumption>)_newline_(hasHypothesis    <experiment> <hypothesis>)_newline_(hasResults       <experiment> <results>)_newline_(hasConclusion    <experiment> <conclusion>)_newline__newline__newline_6     Representing Results as Structured Measurement Objects_newline_6.1   Why not just store a CSV?_newline_Raw tabular storage lacks explicit column semantics (metric identity), units and aggregation rules,_newline_provenance linking each number to a method and dataset, and machine-checkable compatibility_newline_across experiments. Structured measurement objects supply all four._newline__newline_6.2   A reusable measurement schema_newline_Result tables are represented as MeasurementTables containing MeasurementRows, each row holding_newline_multiple Measurement objects:_newline__newline_(instance table7 exmeta:MeasurementTable)_newline_(part table7 exp1)_newline_(tableTitle table7 _quote_Crypto: daily regime statistics_quote_)_newline_(tableHasRow table7 row7.R0)_newline_(tableHasRow table7 row7.R1)_newline_..._newline__newline_(instance row7.R0 exmeta:MeasurementRow)_newline_(rowContext row7.R0 (regime R0) (asset_pool crypto12) (freq daily))_newline_(rowHasMeasurement row7.R0 m7.R0.n_days)_newline_(rowHasMeasurement row7.R0 m7.R0.sigma_d)_newline_(rowHasMeasurement row7.R0 m7.R0.absret)_newline_(rowHasMeasurement row7.R0 m7.R0.persistence)_newline__newline_(instance m7.R0.sigma_d exmeta:Measurement)_newline_(measureMetric m7.R0.sigma_d exfin:DailyVolatility)_newline_(measureValue m7.R0.sigma_d 3.23)_newline_(measureUnit   m7.R0.sigma_d _quote_pct_per_day_quote_)_newline__newline_    A measurement can optionally include an uncertainty object:_newline_(measureValue       m8.R5.llc_lambda 0.883)_newline_(measureUncertainty m8.R5.llc_lambda (stderr 0.078))_newline__newline__newline_7     Epistemic Annotations for Experiment Claims_newline_7.1   Claims versus measurements_newline_Measurements are data objects—numbers together with their meaning, units, and provenance._newline_Claims are propositions derived from measurement sets, possibly under additional modeling as-_newline__newline_                                                5_newline_sumptions. We therefore maintain three distinct object types: Measurement objects, Claim_newline_objects (logical propositions that can be supported or refuted), and EvidenceState objects (epis-_newline_temic annotations attached to claims)._newline__newline_7.2    Evidence states as p-bits_newline_Each claim receives an evidence pair (p, n) with p, n ∈ [0, 1], where p is the degree of positive_newline_support and n is the degree of counter-evidence. Both can be nonzero simultaneously, enabling_newline_paraconsistent accumulation of conflicting results._newline__newline_7.3    Computing (p, n) in practice_newline_A simple (and replaceable) recipe:_newline_    1. Convert effect sizes and uncertainties into a support score—for example, via a sigmoid of_newline_       (estimate / standard error)._newline_    2. Convert known threats to validity (data leakage, non-stationarity, untested assumptions) into_newline_       negative support._newline_    3. Track provenance: record which measurements and assumptions contributed._newline_An illustrative encoding:_newline_(instance claim.crypto.R5.compressible exmeta:Claim)_newline_(claimText claim.crypto.R5.compressible_newline_  _quote_In crypto, Complex Trend (R5) is compressible (positive LLC)._quote_)_newline__newline_(instance ev.claim.crypto.R5.compressible exmeta:EvidenceState)_newline_(evidencePair ev.claim.crypto.R5.compressible (p 0.98) (n 0.05))_newline_(supportedBy claim.crypto.R5.compressible m8.R5.llc_lambda)_newline_(supportedBy claim.crypto.R5.compressible m8.R5.llc_stderr)_newline_(evidenceOf ev.claim.crypto.R5.compressible claim.crypto.R5.compressible)_newline__newline__newline_8     Worked Example: Compression-Based Regime Classification_newline_Appendix A presents a complete, step-by-step formalization of a regime-classification methodology_newline_paper in quantitative finance (Goertzel, 2026). The paper proposes a compression-based approach_newline_to classifying financial time-series into six regimes (three complexity levels crossed with two trend_newline_levels) and evaluates it on 12 cryptocurrency and 14 FX/commodity instruments._newline_    The formalization demonstrates the following key aspects of the representation:_newline__newline_Experiment skeleton. The paper is registered as an EXPO ComputationalExperiment with_newline_explicit goals (computing regime posteriors; investigating cross-asset stability), four research hy-_newline_potheses, and a full parts hierarchy (design, model, results, conclusions)._newline__newline_Explicit factor enumeration. Every experimental “knob” is declared as a Factor with typed_newline_FactorLevels: asset universes (two pools), sampling frequency, symbolization parameters (5×3×3_newline_= 45-symbol encoder), the six-regime taxonomy (complexity × trend axes with pooled-tercile and_newline_median thresholds), LZ78 dictionary capacity, posterior sharpening parameter β, EM refinement_newline_passes, classification window size, and LLC regression windows._newline__newline_                                                  6_newline_Measurement tables. Fourteen result tables from the paper are encoded as MeasurementTable_newline_objects with explicit metrics, units, and provenance—including EM convergence diagnostics, label_newline_distributions, per-instrument regime distributions and confidence scores, daily regime statistics_newline_(volatility, returns, persistence), LLC estimates with standard errors, cross-asset comparisons, and_newline_regime-conditional trading parameter suggestions._newline__newline_Epistemic annotations. Five principal claims are extracted and annotated with p-bit evidence_newline_states: that crypto inhabits all six regimes with high balance (p = 0.93, n = 0.10); that Complex_newline_Trend is uniquely compressible in crypto (p = 0.98, n = 0.05); that no FX/commodity regime is_newline_compressible under the current estimator (p = 0.90, n = 0.12); that cross-asset structure differs_newline_materially (p = 0.92, n = 0.10); and that trading-strategy estimates are indicative only, lacking full_newline_backtest validation (p = 0.95, n = 0.05)._newline__newline__newline_9     How This Enables Search, Analysis, and Reasoning_newline_Once multiple experiments are represented in this framework, MeTTa/PLN-style reasoning can_newline_exploit the structure in several ways:_newline__newline_     • Search by factor level. Find all experiments using a 45-symbol encoder and a 200-bar_newline_       classification window._newline__newline_     • Cross-domain outcome comparison. Treat tables uniformly as measurement objects and_newline_       aggregate metrics across domains._newline__newline_     • Contradiction detection. If two experiments make incompatible claims about the same_newline_       proposition, their evidence states can be combined without explosion (paraconsistent evidence_newline_       pairs)._newline__newline_     • Guided inference. Rules can map result patterns to suggest