wline_6 Domain examples                                                                                   7_newline_  6.1 AI ethics example: policy compliance and value conflicts . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      7_newline_  6.2 Crypto-finance example: on-chain pattern recognition + causal explanations . . . . .          8_newline_  6.3 Biology example: metabolic pathways as pattern-flow networks . . . . . . . . . . . .          9_newline_  6.4 Biology example: causal explanations for genomics data . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        9_newline__newline_7 Suggested MVP experiments                                                                         10_newline__newline_8 Summary                                                                                           11_newline__newline__newline_1     High-level architecture_newline_The basic idea is to build a three-layer stack :_newline__newline_L1: Typed MeTTa-SUMO core. Port SUMO KIF axioms into a MeTTa representation with_newline_    explicit type signatures for classes, relations, functions, and meta-relations (e.g. domain,_newline_    subclass)._newline__newline_L2: Evidence and inference layer (PLN-friendly). Represent semantic parsing outputs and_newline_    dataset facts as evidence-bearing assertions (potentially uncertain, potentially conflicting)._newline_    Use type constraints and SUMO axioms to guide inference and control search._newline__newline_L3: SUMO ↔ Hyperseed bridge. Represent a mapping from SUMO symbols and structures_newline_    (classes, relations, patterns) into Hyperseed concepts and structures, using graded correspon-_newline_    dences and explicit contexts. Use this bridge to: (a) interpret domain facts in a Hyperseed_newline_    framing, (b) use Hyperseed concepts as inference guidance heuristics and explanations, and_newline_    (c) translate back into SUMO-level statements when needed for application output._newline__newline__newline_2     Representation choices_newline_2.1   Two levels of meaning: object-level vs meta-level_newline_SUMO is mostly first-order but includes meta-predicates about predicates/classes. MeTTa can_newline_represent both, but it is helpful to explicitly separate:_newline__newline_    • Object-level statements about individuals, e.g. (parent John Mary)._newline__newline_    • Meta-level statements about symbols, e.g. domain/range declarations or talking about a_newline_      predicate as a first-class entity._newline__newline_    To support this, define two parallel encodings:_newline__newline_ (a) Direct application form (fast, type-checkable):_newline_     (P a1 ... an) where P is a relation symbol with a declared signature._newline__newline__newline_                                                   2_newline_(b) Reified form (meta-reasoning-friendly):_newline_    (Holds P (Args a1 ... an)) where P is treated as a term._newline__newline_    A bridge rule links them (conceptually, not necessarily as an always-fired rewrite):_newline__newline_                          (P a1 . . . an ) ↔ Holds(P, Args(a1 , . . . , an ))._newline__newline_Keeping both encodings available makes it easier to do higher-order manipulation without sacrificing_newline_efficient type checking on ordinary facts._newline__newline_2.2   Namespaces_newline_In practice, use namespaces to avoid collisions:_newline__newline_    • SUMO:: for ported SUMO symbols._newline__newline_    • HS:: for Hyperseed concepts/symbols._newline__newline_    • DOM:: for domain-specific extensions (ethics/finance/bio)._newline__newline__newline_3     A higher-order type system for MeTTa-SUMO_newline_3.1   Core type universe_newline_We want types rich enough to express: (i) classes and subclassing, (ii) relation signatures, (iii)_newline_meta-relations about relations, and (iv) contextual and graded truth._newline_   A minimal starting point:_newline_; Meta-types (kinds)_newline_(: Type       Type)_newline_(: Class      Type)_newline_(: RelSym     Type)_newline_(: FunSym     Type)_newline_(: Prop       Type)_newline_(: Context    Type)_newline_(: Evidence   Type)_newline__newline_; Some useful ground types_newline_(: Entity     Type)_newline_(: Nat        Type)_newline_    Then define how SUMO-level symbols live in this universe:_newline_; SUMO class symbols are of type Class_newline_(: SUMO::Human        Class)_newline_(: SUMO::Organization Class)_newline__newline_; SUMO relation symbols are of type RelSym_newline_(: SUMO::parent       RelSym)_newline_(: SUMO::agent        RelSym)_newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                   3_newline_3.2   Relation signatures (domain/range as typing)_newline_For each SUMO relation R, generate a signature:_newline_; A signature object says what argument types a relation expects._newline_(: Sig Type)_newline_(: mkSig (-> Nat (List Type) Type Sig))_newline__newline_; Example: parent : Human x Human -> Prop_newline_(: sig:SUMO::parent Sig)_newline_(= sig:SUMO::parent (mkSig 2 (List Entity Entity) Prop))_newline_    In real ports, you derive these signatures from SUMO domain/range axioms (and from conven-_newline_tions for functional terms)._newline__newline_3.3   Subtyping and class-as-type discipline_newline_Treat SUMO subclassing as subtyping:_newline_(: Subclass (-> Class Class Prop))_newline_(: Subtype (-> Type Type Prop))_newline__newline_; Bridging rule (conceptual): a SUMO subclass edge induces a type-subtype edge._newline_; (Subclass A B) ==> (Subtype (TypeOf A) (TypeOf B))_newline_   There are multiple ways to implement TypeOf. A pragmatic approach: create a distinct type_newline_atom for each class:_newline_(: TypeOf (-> Class Type))_newline__newline_(: T:Human        Type)_newline_(: T:Organization Type)_newline_(= (TypeOf SUMO::Human)        T:Human)_newline_(= (TypeOf SUMO::Organization) T:Organization)_newline_   Then instances can be typed:_newline_(: John   T:Human)_newline_(: Acme   T:Organization)_newline__newline__newline_3.4   Typed application rules_newline_A type checker can implement the following judgments._newline__newline_Term typing._newline_                                           Γ`a:T_newline_meaning term a has type T in environment Γ._newline__newline_Relation application. If R has signature (T1,...,Tn) -> Prop and each ai has type Ti (or a_newline_subtype), then (R a1 ... an) is well-typed._newline__newline_Subtyping.    Use standard subsumption:_newline_                                       Γ`a:T T ≤U_newline_                                         Γ`a:U_newline__newline_                                              4_newline_   3.5    Context-indexed truth and evidence_newline_   To support PLN-like and Hyperseed-like workflows, represent evidence explicitly._newline_      A minimal pattern:_newline_   ; Evidence object (can be PLN stv, p-bit, or a wrapper that holds both)_newline_   (: TV Type)_newline_   (: stv (-> Number Number TV))      ; (strength, confidence)_newline_   (: pbit (-> Number Number TV))     ; (pos, neg)_newline__newline_   (: Assert (-> Context Prop TV Prop))_newline_   ; Assert(C, P, tv) is itself a proposition in the KB, enabling meta-reasoning._newline_       You can then store:_newline_   (: C:text    Context)_newline_   (: C:data    Context)_newline__newline_   ; Parsed from a sentence with uncertainty:_newline_   (Assert C:text (SUMO::parent John Mary) (stv 0.72 0.45))_newline__newline_   ; From a structured dataset with higher confidence:_newline_   (Assert C:data (SUMO::parent John Mary) (stv 0.95 0.90))_newline__newline__newline_   3.6    Formal type-checking passes_newline_   A realistic pipeline uses multiple passes:_newline__newline_Pass 1: Symbol hygiene pass: every symbol used is declared (or auto-declared into a quarantine_newline_        namespace)._newline__newline_Pass 2: Arity pass: every (R ...) application matches the declared arity._newline__newline_Pass 3: Signature pass: argument types satisfy relation signature types (up to subtyping)._newline__newline_Pass 4: Meta-axiom pass: meta-relations like domain, range, subclass are applied to appropriate_newline_        meta-types (e.g. a RelSym where expected)._newline__newline_Pass 5: Disjointness and contradiction pass: identify contradictions such as: Disjoint(A,B)_newline_        plus x:A and x:B. Decide whether these are treated as: (a) hard errors (strict mode), or (b)_newline_        paraconsistent tensions (graded mode)._newline__newline__newline_   4     Using SUMO for PLN reasoning on semantic parses + datasets_newline_   4.1    Why SUMO helps_newline_   Semantic parsing from text tends to yield:_newline__newline_       • many candidate predicates,_newline__newline_       • many ambiguous entity types,_newline__newline_       • many missing implicit arguments, and_newline__newline_       • nontrivial uncertainty._newline__newline_                                                   5_newline_   A typed SUMO backbone helps by providing:_newline__newline_   • type-guided disambiguation (only a few predicate choices will type-check),_newline__newline_   • search control (only inference paths consistent with domains/ranges are explored),_newline__newline_   • abductive structure (SUMO axioms suggest what missing facts could explain an observa-_newline_     tion),_newline__newline_   • data fusion hooks (dataset fields map into well-typed predicates)._newline__newline_4.2   PLN-friendly rule templates derived from SUMO_newline_Many SUMO axioms compile into reusable inference templates._newline__newline__newline_; If x is an instance of A, and A is a subclass of B, then x is an instance of B._newline_(: Instance (-> Entity Class Prop))_newline__newline_(rule_newline_  (and (Instance $x $A) (Subclass $A $B))_newline_  (Instance $x $B))_newline__newline__newline__newline_Domain/range enforcement as soft inference. Instead of treating domain/range violations_newline_as immediate rejection, you can treat them as evidence signals:_newline__newline_   • If R(a,b) is asserted but a is not typed as the domain class, infer either: (a) a type correction_newline_     candidate for a, or (b) negative evidence against R(a,b)._newline__newline_4.3   Combining evidence from text and data_newline_Maintain multiple contexts and then combine them in a controlled way:_newline__newline_   • C:text: semantic parses, subjective reports, policy documents._newline__newline_   • C:data: structured datasets, extracted graphs, measured signals._newline__newline_   • C:model: consequences derived from SUMO axioms + PLN inference._newline__newline_   You can implement aggregation rules such as:_newline__newline_   • merge evidence when two assertions unify,_newline__newline_   • promote conclusions when multiple independent sources agree,_newline__newline_   • preserve conflicts explicitly rather than collapsing them._newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                  6_newline_5     Connecting SUMO to Hyperseed_newline_5.1     What the SUMO ↔ Hyperseed bridge should be_newline_For practical use, the bridge should not be a brittle one-to-one mapping. It should be a graded_newline_correspondence that:_newline__newline_     • can say “SUMO::Trust is close to HS::Trustworthiness” without claiming identity,_newline__newline_     • is context-sensitive (a legal trust vs a cryptographic trust vs a social trust),_newline__newline_     • composes (bridges can be chained), and_newline__newline_     • can be used both as an interpretation tool and as inference guidance._newline__newline_5.2     Representing mappings as graded correspondences_newline_Define a bridge object:_newline_(: Bridge Type)_newline_(: mkBridge (-> Symbol Symbol Context TV Bridge))_newline__newline_; _quote_SUMO symbol S corresponds to Hyperseed symbol H in context C with degree tv_quote__newline_(: Corresponds (-> Symbol Symbol Context TV Prop))_newline_     Examples:_newline_(: C:ethics Context)_newline_(: C:crypto Context)_newline_(: C:bio     Context)_newline__newline_(Corresponds SUMO::Process            HS::Process      C:bio    (pbit 0.90 0.05))_newline_(Corresponds SUMO::Agreement          HS::Communication C:ethics (pbit 0.70 0.20))_newline_(Corresponds SUMO::Program            HS::Program      C:crypto (pbit 0.85 0.10))_newline__newline__newline_5.3     Using the bridge for inference guidance_newline_Given a SUMO-level assertion P, do:_newline__newline_    1. Find Hyperseed concepts that correspond to the main predicates/classes in P._newline__newline_    2. Use Hyperseed-specific inference rules or heuristics (e.g. context shifts, pattern flow, value_newline_       paraconsistency) to propose:_newline__newline_         • additional latent variables to consider,_newline_         • likely missing facts,_newline_         • plausible causal explanations,_newline_         • which contradictions are “allowed tensions” vs “needs repair”._newline__newline_    3. Translate any resulting hypotheses back into SUMO-level candidates, then type-check and_newline_       test them against data._newline__newline_  This makes Hyperseed a semantic and methodological compass rather than a replacement for_newline_SUMO._newline__newline_                                                      7_newline_6     Domain examples_newline_6.1    AI ethics example: policy compliance and value conflicts_newline_Scenario. Text: “Company Acme deployed facial recognition in public spaces without consent.”_newline_   Semantic parsing yields uncertain candidates (illustrative):_newline_(Assert C:text_newline_  (SUMO::agent DOM::DeployAction Acme)_newline_  (stv 0.8 0.5))_newline__newline_(Assert C:text_newline_  (SUMO::patient DOM::DeployAction DOM::FacialRecognitionSystem)_newline_  (stv 0.7 0.5))_newline__newline_(Assert C:text_newline_  (DOM::inContext DOM::FacialRecognitionSystem DOM::PublicSpace)_newline_  (stv 0.7 0.4))_newline__newline_(Assert C:text_newline_  (DOM::hasConsent DOM::DeployAction DOM::NoConsent)_newline_  (stv 0.6 0.4))_newline__newline__newline_SUMO guidance. Type constraints can quickly rule out mis-parses, e.g. agent expects an_newline_Entity of an appropriate type; patient expects an object._newline__newline_Hyperseed guidance.        Map the situation into Hyperseed ethical notions and allow paraconsis-_newline_tent values:_newline__newline_    • one can have evidence both for “improves safety” and “violates privacy”;_newline__newline_    • represent this as a structured, non-explosive tension rather than forcing collapse._newline__newline_    Illustrative Hyperseed-oriented derived atoms:_newline_; These are not claimed SUMO facts; they are HS-framed interpretations._newline_(Assert C:model_newline_  (HS::ValueImpact DOM::DeployAction HS::Safety)_newline_  (pbit 0.60 0.20))_newline__newline_(Assert C:model_newline_  (HS::ValueImpact DOM::DeployAction HS::Autonomy)_newline_  (pbit 0.15 0.70))_newline__newline_(Assert C:model_newline_  (HS::ValueImpact DOM::DeployAction HS::Compassion)_newline_  (pbit 0.10 0.65))_newline_   Then reasoning can seek policies or actions that satisfy constraints under conflict, rather than_newline_expecting a single scalar utility._newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                 8_newline_6.2   Crypto-finance example: on-chain pattern recognition + causal explana-_newline_      tions_newline_Assumptions. We assume: (i) access to crypto datasets (transactions, addresses, contract events),_newline_(ii) tools that recognize patterns (wash trading, mixers, sandwich attacks, etc.)._newline__newline_Data ingestion into SUMO.        Represent a transaction and its roles:_newline_(: Tx123 Entity)_newline_(: Alice Entity)_newline_(: Bob   Entity)_newline_(: TokenX Entity)_newline__newline_(Assert C:data (DOM::isTransaction Tx123) (stv 0.99 0.95))_newline_(Assert C:data (DOM::sender Tx123 Alice) (stv 0.99 0.95))_newline_(Assert C:data (DOM::receiver Tx123 Bob) (stv 0.99 0.95)