NTRUSTED extracted_by=pdftotext>>>_newline_                       Weakness, Singular Learning Theory,_newline_                           and Punctuated Equilibrium_newline_                      in Evolutionary Algorithms and EDAs_newline_                                              Ben Goertzel_newline__newline_                                           December 6, 2025_newline__newline__newline_                                                 Abstract_newline_         We develop a conceptual and technical link between weakness-based schema theorems, singular_newline_     learning theory (SLT), and the dynamics of evolutionary algorithms (EAs) and estimation of_newline_     distribution algorithms (EDAs). Weakness is used as a compositional fitness/Occam measure;_newline_     SLT provides the local learning coefficient (LLC) that quantifies degeneracy of minima via the_newline_     exponent of local evidence. In the infinite-population limit, we interpret evolutionary dynamics as_newline_     gradient flows of a free-energy functional, i.e. minimal-free-energy (minimal-weakness) geodesics_newline_     in population or parameter space. We then show how qualitative “stages” of evolution, including_newline_     punctuated-equilibrium-like behaviour, can be interpreted as SLT phase changes between different_newline_     singular strata, each with its own LLC._newline_         We apply this viewpoint to genetic algorithms (GAs) and EDAs on deceptive trap landscapes._newline_     For a factorized (univariate) EDA on a simple 2-bit trap, we explicitly compute the free energy_newline_     and examine the Hessian near deceptive and global optima. We then discuss a 4-bit trap for_newline_     both factorized and block-factor (dependency-aware) EDAs, explaining how the latter changes_newline_     the SLT free-energy landscape so that the global basin becomes thermodynamically preferred._newline_     This provides a geometric SLT explanation of why dependency-aware EDAs are less susceptible_newline_     to deception. Finally, we discuss the large-but-finite population case and identify conditions_newline_     under which infinite-population SLT/weakness conclusions remain qualitatively valid._newline__newline__newline_Contents_newline_1 Introduction                                                                                             2_newline__newline_2 Background                                                                                               3_newline_  2.1 Evolutionary algorithms and EDAs . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .             3_newline_  2.2 Weakness and schema theorems . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .             3_newline_  2.3 Singular learning theory and local learning coefficient . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          4_newline__newline_3 Weakness and Evolution as Free-Energy Geodesics                                                          4_newline_  3.1 Infinite-population GA as a gradient flow . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .            4_newline_  3.2 Weakness schema theorem as a discrete SLT shadow . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .                 5_newline__newline_4 SLT for Evolutionary Attractors and Punctuated Equilibrium                                               5_newline_  4.1 Population-as-parameter SLT . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .            6_newline_  4.2 Stage transitions as SLT phase flips . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         6_newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                     1_newline_5 Factorized EDA on Deceptive Traps: A Concrete Analysis                                              6_newline_  5.1 Univariate EDA on a 2-bit deceptive trap . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        6_newline_  5.2 Factorized EDA on a 4-bit trap (qualitative) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        9_newline__newline_6 Dependency-Aware EDAs: Phase Flip in the SLT Landscape                                         9_newline_  6.1 Block-factor EDA on a 4-bit trap . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 9_newline_  6.2 SLT/weakness perspective: a phase flip . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 10_newline__newline_7 Finite Population Effects: When Do Infinite-Pop Conclusions Hold?                                 10_newline_  7.1 Noise scaling in finite populations . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 10_newline_  7.2 Selection vs drift and SLT basins . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 11_newline__newline_8 Discussion                                                                                         11_newline__newline_9 Conclusion                                                                                         12_newline__newline__newline_1     Introduction_newline_The aim of this paper is to connect three strands of theory that are usually studied separately:_newline__newline_    • Evolutionary algorithms and EDAs, which evolve large populations of candidate solutions by_newline_      selection, mutation, crossover, and probabilistic modeling._newline__newline_    • Weakness, a compositional Occam-like evaluation combining fitness and simplicity in a way_newline_      that interacts well with schema theorems._newline__newline_    • Singular learning theory (SLT), which characterizes how Bayesian evidence behaves near singular_newline_      minima and introduces the local learning coefficient (LLC) as a measure of degeneracy._newline__newline_    At an intuitive level, evolutionary search in a large population is a process that finds and refines_newline_families of solutions, not isolated points. There is typically a large amount of redundancy: many_newline_distinct genotypes have essentially the same phenotypic effect or fitness. This redundancy is exactly_newline_the sort of degeneracy that SLT studies. At the same time, weakness supplies an Occam bias_newline_favoring more compressible, more reusable patterns._newline_    Our guiding slogan is:_newline__newline_       Infinite-population evolutionary learning of weak models follows minimal-free-energy_newline_       (minimal-weakness) geodesics, and its developmental stages correspond to SLT phase_newline_       transitions between singular strata with different LLC, giving a mathematically grounded_newline_       version of punctuated equilibrium._newline__newline_    We explore this in two concrete settings: a bitstring genetic algorithm (GA) with selection,_newline_mutation, and crossover; and simple EDAs in both factorized and dependency-aware forms. On_newline_deceptive trap landscapes, we show how the SLT/weakness geometry of the free-energy landscape_newline_differs between factorized and block-factor EDAs, and why the latter are structurally better at_newline_escaping deception._newline_    We begin with a brief review of evolutionary algorithms, weakness, and SLT, then construct_newline_a free-energy picture of evolution, and finally work through deceptive-trap examples for EDAs in_newline_some detail._newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                   2_newline_2     Background_newline_The goal of this section is to spell out the basic ingredients we will use later: evolutionary algorithms_newline_and EDAs, the weakness perspective on schema theorems, and the SLT notion of local evidence and_newline_LLC._newline__newline_2.1     Evolutionary algorithms and EDAs_newline_We consider bitstring genotypes x ∈ {0, 1}N . A simple GA evolves a population of Npop individuals_newline_over discrete generations:_newline__newline_    • Selection: choose parents with probability proportional to a fitness (or weakness) score w(x)._newline__newline_    • Crossover: recombine parent bitstrings using k-point crossover._newline__newline_    • Mutation: flip each bit with probability µ._newline__newline_     In the infinite-population limit, the population can be represented by a frequency distribution_newline_pt (x) over genotypes, and the dynamics become deterministic. Under suitable scaling, this can_newline_be approximated by a replicator–mutator equation, which we will view as a gradient flow of a_newline_free-energy functional._newline_     An estimation-of-distribution algorithm (EDA) maintains and updates a parametric distribution_newline_qθ (x) over genotypes. At each generation:_newline__newline_ 1. Sample a population x(1) , . . . , x(M ) from qθ ._newline__newline_ 2. Select individuals based on w(x) (e.g. top-α or stochastic selection)._newline__newline_ 3. Fit a new parameter θ0 (e.g. maximum likelihood) to the selected set._newline__newline_In the infinite-sample limit, the θ-update becomes a deterministic mapping that we can describe as_newline_gradient descent on a suitable free-energy functional._newline_    We will use two EDA families:_newline_                                                             QN     xi         1−xi ._newline_    • Factorized EDA: assumes independent bits, qθ (x) =       i=1 θi (1 − θi )_newline__newline_    • Block-factor EDA: groups bits into blocks b and models each block distribution qb (xb ), so_newline_              Q_newline_      qΘ (x) = b qb (xb )._newline__newline_2.2     Weakness and schema theorems_newline_Weakness is a way of assigning a real-valued cost or “badness” to a program or genotype that is_newline_compatible with compositional structures and schema theorems. For this paper we do not need the_newline_full categorical formalism; it suffices to note that we can:_newline__newline_    • treat w(x) as a weakness score, lower is better or higher is better depending on convention;_newline__newline_    • interpret schemata as coarse descriptors of bit patterns;_newline__newline_    • and reason about expected schema frequencies over generations under selection, mutation, and_newline_      crossover._newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                         3_newline_     Classical schema theorems state that certain schemata with above-average fitness proliferate_newline_exponentially in expectation, if crossover and mutation do not disrupt them too much. When_newline_weakness is used as the fitness signal, similar results hold: schemata representing more compressible,_newline_more reusable patterns are favoured._newline_     From the SLT perspective, the size of a schema class (how many concrete genotypes instantiate_newline_it) is a discrete proxy for the volume of near-optimal parameters. In the continuous analysis that_newline_follows, we will replace schema counts with actual volume integrals in parameter space._newline__newline_2.3     Singular learning theory and local learning coefficient_newline_SLT focuses on the behavior of Bayesian evidence in singular models. Given a loss (or negative_newline_log-likelihood) `(θ), a prior density φ(θ), and n data points, the (local) evidence integral is_newline_                                           Z_newline_                                    Zn =       exp(−n`n (θ)) φ(θ) dθ,_newline__newline_where `n is the empirical loss. Near a minimizer θ∗ , and under analyticity assumptions, SLT shows_newline_that_newline_                           − log Zn = n`n (θ∗ ) + λ(θ∗ ) log n + O(log log n).                  (1)_newline_The coefficient λ(θ∗ ) is called the local learning coefficient (LLC). It plays the role of an effective_newline_model complexity:_newline__newline_    • in regular models of dimension d, λ = d/2;_newline__newline_    • in singular models, λ may be fractional and is determined by the singularity structure (e.g._newline_      manifolds of minima, symmetries)._newline__newline_    Equivalently, one can look at sublevel sets of the excess loss K(θ) = `(θ) − `(θ∗ ) ≥ 0. Define the_newline_local volume                         