y attractors._newline__newline__newline_4     SLT for Evolutionary Attractors and Punctuated Equilibrium_newline_In this section we interpret evolutionary attractors as minima of a free-energy functional and_newline_qualitatively import SLT’s notion of LLC to them. We then connect evolutionary stage changes to_newline_phase flips between basins._newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                       5_newline_4.1     Population-as-parameter SLT_newline_Instead of treating θ as parameters, we can treat the population distribution p itself as an unknown_newline_element of a manifold P, e.g. the simplex of distributions over a finite space. The free-energy_newline_functional F [p] plays the role of loss._newline_    In principle, one can apply SLT to F [p] just as to F (θ): consider a parameterization of P (for_newline_instance, via sufficient statistics) and analyse the local volume of distributions near a minimizer p∗ ,_newline_                                        {p : F [p] ≤ F [p∗ ] + ε}._newline_If the minimizer is regular and isolated, the local geometry is quadratic and the corresponding_newline_LLC is (d/2), where d is the dimension of the parameterization. If there are neutral directions_newline_(continuous families of equally good distributions), the LLC is smaller, reflecting degeneracy._newline_    Thus, in an evo-dynamics context, one can interpret:_newline_    • lower LLC at p∗ as a broader, more redundant attractor, with many almost-equivalent population_newline_      states;_newline_    • higher LLC as a stiffer attractor with fewer neutral directions._newline__newline_4.2     Stage transitions as SLT phase flips_newline_Suppose there are multiple local minima p∗i of F [p], corresponding to different evolutionary “macro-_newline_organizations” (different ecotypes, modes, or modular structures). In SLT, the contribution of each_newline_minimum to evidence involves both the free energy and the LLC. If one had an effective sample size_newline_n (for example, interpreting n as the amount of fitness information or the length of an evolutionary_newline_episode), one could write a schematic expansion_newline_                                      Fi (n) ≈ nF [p∗i ] + λi log n._newline_As n increases, the tradeoff between the leading term nFi and the subleading λi log n can cause the_newline_identity of the most thermodynamically favourable basin to change. At moderate scales, a slightly_newline_worse free-energy minimum with very low LLC (fat basin) may dominate; at large scales, a slightly_newline_better free-energy minimum with higher LLC may take over._newline_    In evolutionary terms, this is reminiscent of punctuated equilibrium: long periods where the_newline_population fluctuates near one attractor, followed by relatively rapid transitions to a different_newline_attractor when the balance of fit vs degeneracy changes due to environmental change or internal_newline_structural discovery. The SLT language clarifies this as a phase flip between singular strata._newline__newline__newline_5     Factorized EDA on Deceptive Traps: A Concrete Analysis_newline_We now step into a concrete setting where we can write formulas explicitly: a univariate (factorized)_newline_EDA on a deceptive trap landscape. We first work out a 2-bit case in detail and then discuss the_newline_4-bit trap qualitatively._newline__newline_5.1     Univariate EDA on a 2-bit deceptive trap_newline_We consider bitstrings x = (x1 , x2 ) ∈ {0, 1}2 and a simple deceptive trap function. Define the trap_newline_via the number of ones u(x) = x1 + x2 :_newline_                                             (_newline_                                             2         if u = 2,_newline_                                   f (u) =_newline_                                             2 − 1 − u if u = 0, 1._newline__newline_                                                    6_newline_Thus_newline_                   