_newline_\newcommand{\E}{\mathbb{E}}_newline_\newcommand{\R}{\mathbb{R}}_newline_\newcommand{\Vol}{\mathrm{Vol}}_newline_\newcommand{\bits}{\mathrm{bits}}_newline_\newcommand{\code}{\mathrm{CL}}  % code length (MDL-ish)_newline_\newcommand{\weak}{\mathrm{W}}  % weakness value in (0,1] or in a quantale_newline_\newcommand{\Zev}{\mathcal{Z}}  % evidence / partition function_newline_\newcommand{\Ffree}{\mathcal{F}}% free energy / MDL score_newline_\newcommand{\ind}{\mathbf{1}}   % indicator_newline__newline_\newtheorem{definition}{Definition}_newline_\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}_newline_\newtheorem{remark}{Remark}_newline__newline_\title{ {\huge Detecting Regime Changes\\ in Hierarchical Data Streams\\ via Singular Learning Theory_newline_and Quantale Weakness} \\ (applied to Incremental Compression and \\ Causal/Predictive Coding Neural Models\\_newline_in Hierarchical L\_apostrophe_evy Jump Regimes)}_newline_\author{Ben Goertzel}_newline_\date{\today}_newline__newline_\begin{document}_newline_\maketitle_newline__newline_\begin{abstract}_newline_Incremental compression (IC) and causal-coding / predictive-coding (CC/PC) neural models_newline_both try to exploit hierarchical structure in data by building reusable modules (features, submodels,_newline_or parameter blocks) plus residual coupling._newline_In stationary settings, refitting-based IC can recover one feature per hierarchical level and achieve_newline_near-optimal two-part description length; similarly, CC/PC with causal gates and pruning can drive_newline_a network toward a causally modular parameterization where context updates almost commute,_newline_mitigating catastrophic forgetting._newline__newline_In streaming settings, however, the underlying hierarchical generator can switch regimes._newline_This paper proposes a regime-change detector and adaptation rule using singular learning theory (SLT)_newline_parameters---especially per-module local learning coefficients (LLCs)---as diagnostics for when the_newline_source distribution has changed and at which hierarchical levels. We use quantale weakness as_newline_compositional ``glue_apostrophe__apostrophe_ by identifying weakness with (local) evidence / partition functions, so that_newline_module-wise evidence multiplies (free energies add) and LLCs add \emph{under a dominated-interaction_newline_approximate-factorization condition}, enabling level-localizing change hypotheses to be composed algebraically._newline__newline_We present (i) a unified regime signature for IC and CC/PC that tracks per-module LLCs, gain/cost_newline_profiles, and (for CC/PC) locality/commutator proxies; (ii) an SLT-aware MDL change-point test_newline_with an interpretable $\lambda \log n$ complexity penalty; (iii) a weakness-compositional localization_newline_rule selecting which modules changed; and (iv) selective-refit / selective-gate adaptation policies._newline_As a concrete example, we treat hierarchical generative sources built from L\_apostrophe_evy processes with jumps_newline_(e.g. stable or compound-Poisson components) and show how transitions between L\_apostrophe_evy triplets_newline_can be interpreted as multi-level regime changes (e.g. financial market regime shifts).   We also argue that these_newline_SLT based signals give indication of whether TransWeave-based transfer learning of RL models from one regime to another is likely to succeed or fail, in the strong (near-isomorphic) sense._newline_\end{abstract}_newline__newline_\section{Motivation}_newline_Incremental compression is attractive when data exhibits compositional structure:_newline_edges compose into textures, textures into objects, objects into scenes; characters into words_newline_into phrases into sentences. Predictive-coding style hierarchies are attractive for similar reasons:_newline_inference learns layered latent representations and error signals._newline__newline_In a streaming setting, the key failure mode is regime change: data starts coming from a different_newline_hierarchical generator (or mixture), so that some modules become mismatched._newline_We ask:_newline_\begin{enumerate}_newline_  \item Can SLT provide reliable indicators of regime change, beyond heuristic drift signals?_newline_  \item Can the indicators be level-specific in hierarchical settings?_newline_  \item Can weakness provide a compositional language for combining module-wise evidence,_newline_        so that we both \emph{detect} and \emph{localize} changes?_newline_  \item Can the same machinery cover both (a) incremental compression algorithms and_newline_        (b) CC/PC neural learners that implement causal modularization?_newline_        \item Can we use this sort of regime change indicator to tell us whether RL-based models trained on one regime can be viably transfer-learned to another?_newline_\end{enumerate}_newline__newline_\section{Background}_newline_\subsection{Incremental compression (IC)}_newline_We view IC as producing a two-part code: a list of feature programs plus a final residual._newline__newline_\begin{definition}[Feature and incremental decomposition]_newline_Fix a string $x \in \{0,1\}^N$. A \emph{feature} is an invertible, length-preserving program $f$._newline_A length-$s$ \emph{incremental decomposition} is a feature sequence_newline_$F=(f_1,\dots,f_s)$ with residuals $r_0 := x$, $r_i := f_i(r_{i-1})$ such that_newline_\[_newline_x = f_1 \circ \cdots \circ f_s(r_s),_newline_\qquad_newline_K(x \mid f_1,\dots,f_s,r_s) = O(\log N)._newline_\]_newline_Define per-step normalized compression and cost_newline_\[_newline_\gamma_i := \frac{K(r_{i-1})-K(r_i)}{N},_newline_\qquad_newline_\tau_i := \frac{K(f_i)}{N},_newline_\]_newline_and the two-part length $L_s := \sum_{i=1}^s K(f_i) + K(r_s)$._newline_\end{definition}_newline__newline_Because $K(\cdot)$ is uncomputable, in practice one substitutes a concrete description language and_newline_uses a code length $\code(\cdot)$ in place of $K(\cdot)$. Weakness theory (Section~\ref{sec:weakness})_newline_provides a principled way to do so while preserving composition laws._newline__newline_\begin{definition}[Decomposition quality metrics]_newline_Define efficiency_newline_\[_newline_\eta(F;x) := \frac{N - L_s}{N - K(x)} \in [0,1],_newline_\]_newline_local margin $\mu := \min_i \frac{\gamma_i-\tau_i}{\gamma_i}$, and balance_newline_$\beta := \frac{\min_i \gamma_i}{\max_i \gamma_i}$._newline_\end{definition}_newline__newline_\subsubsection{Hierarchical sources and refitting}_newline_A short-feature hierarchical generative model (SF-HGM) is a stylized assumption under which IC can succeed:_newline_each level contributes a nontrivial fraction of compressible bits, features remain short (logarithmic description),_newline_and cross-level interactions (mutual information overlap) are bounded. The key algorithmic lesson is:_newline_\emph{refitting} prevents early features from absorbing higher-level structure by allowing earlier features_newline_to relinquish cross-level fragments when a later feature arrives. This suggests a natural streaming adaptation rule:_newline_\emph{reuse what is stable, refit what changes}._newline__newline_\subsection{CC/PC neural models: causal-coding predictive networks}\label{sec:ccpc}_newline_We summarize a CC/PC viewpoint in which predictive coding provides hierarchical inference, while_newline_``causal coding_apostrophe__apostrophe_ encourages modularity by gating and pruning updates based on causal influence._newline__newline_\subsubsection{PC/CC architecture and inference}_newline_Consider a multilayer predictive-coding network with observed input $x \in \R^{d_x}$ and latent layers_newline_$z^1,\dots,z^L$ with vertical weights $W^{\ell+1,\ell}$ and lateral weights $L^{\ell}$. Inference runs a bounded_newline_discrete-time dynamics_newline_\[_newline_z^\ell(t+1) = \Phi_\ell(z^1(t),\dots,z^L(t),x;\theta), \qquad \ell=1,\dots,L,_newline_\]_newline_often derived from gradient descent on a free-energy functional $F(x,z;\theta)$._newline__newline_\subsubsection{Vertical/lateral gating and pruning}_newline_A natural modularization treats each vertical or lateral weight block (matrix, head, channel, kernel)_newline_as a module $\theta^{(m)}$._newline_Introduce multiplicative vertical gates $G^{\ell+1,\ell}[j,i] \in [0,1]$ and effective weights_newline_\[_newline_W^{\ell+1,\ell}_{\mathrm{eff}}[j,i] = G^{\ell+1,\ell}[j,i]\; W^{\ell+1,\ell}[j,i]._newline_\]_newline_For lateral edges, a diffusion-based ``clarity_apostrophe__apostrophe_ penalty shrinks edges whose influence is largely_newline_reproduced by multi-hop paths in the lateral graph (via a Laplacian/heat-kernel redundancy test)._newline_A typical total training objective has the form_newline_\[_newline_L_{\mathrm{total}}(\theta,G) = L_{\mathrm{base}}(\theta) + \sum_{\ell} L_{\mathrm{diff}}^{(\ell)}(L^\ell)_newline_+ L_{\mathrm{match}}(G) + L_{\mathrm{sparse}}(G),_newline_\]_newline_with periodic re-estimation of importance scores from inference trajectories._newline__newline_\subsubsection{Continual learning as (approximate) commutativity}_newline_In nonstationary streams, contexts induce context-conditioned losses $L_c(\theta)$ and gradient vector fields_newline_$X_c(\theta) = -\nabla L_c(\theta)$. Sequential training composes context flows; joint training corresponds to_newline_the flow of the combined field. The difference is governed by commutators (Lie brackets)_newline_\[_newline_[X_c,X_{c_apostrophe_}](\theta) = D X_{c_apostrophe_}(\theta) X_c(\theta) - D X_c(\theta) X_{c_apostrophe_}(\theta),_newline_\]_newline_and in gradient systems can be expressed via mixed Hessian blocks. Causal gates and pruning aim to suppress_newline_cross-module couplings, shrinking mixed blocks and hence commutators._newline__newline_A discrete abstract formulation uses context updates $U_c:\Theta \to \Theta$ on a modularized parameter space,_newline_support sets $S_c$ (modules touched by context $c$), and locality errors controlling leakage outside support._newline_Approximate locality plus small commutator defects imply bounded path dependence and bounded forgetting._newline__newline_\paragraph{Why this matters for regime change.}_newline_A ``regime_apostrophe__apostrophe_ in the data stream corresponds to a context or mixture of contexts._newline_A regime shift is then visible as (i) a change in which modules/edges are causally active (support/gates),_newline_(ii) an increase in leakage/commutator proxies, and (iii) a change in SLT compressibility geometry (LLCs)_newline_of the modules that must now fit different data._newline__newline_\subsection{Singular learning theory (SLT), MDL, and compressibility}_newline_SLT explains why model complexity is not simply parameter count in singular (non-regular) models._newline_Its central geometric object is the volume of low-loss (or low-KL) neighborhoods._newline__newline_\begin{definition}[Sublevel-set volume law and LLC]_newline_Let $K(w) \ge 0$ be a nonnegative analytic ``excess loss_apostrophe__apostrophe_ around a local minimum $w^\ast$._newline_Define the sublevel-set volume_newline_\[_newline_V(\varepsilon) := \Vol\{ w : K(w) \le \varepsilon\}._newline_\]_newline_Under SLT assumptions,_newline_\[_newline_V(\varepsilon) \sim c\, \varepsilon^{\lambda(w^\ast)} \, (-\log \varepsilon)^{m(w^\ast)-1}_newline_\quad\text{as } \varepsilon \to 0,_newline_\]_newline_for rational $\lambda(w^\ast)$ and integer multiplicity $m(w^\ast)$._newline_We call $\lambda(w^\ast)$ the \emph{local learning coefficient (LLC)}._newline_\end{definition}_newline__newline_\paragraph{Quantization/compressibility link.}_newline_In simple grid quantization, the critical per-coordinate bit budget required to keep a whole cell within_newline_a tolerated-loss basin obeys_newline_\[_newline_b^\ast(\varepsilon) \approx \frac{\lambda(w^\ast)}{d} \log_2\!\frac{1}{\varepsilon}_newline_\quad\text{(up to lower-order } \log\log(1/\varepsilon)\text{ terms),}_newline_\]_newline_where $d$ is parameter dimension. Thus, increases in LLC correspond to \emph{less} geometric degeneracy and_newline_\emph{more} bits needed for a fixed tolerance._newline__newline_\paragraph{SLT-MDL redundancy form.}_newline_SLT yields an MDL-style redundancy term of the form $\lambda \log n$ (with multiplicity corrections),_newline_suggesting an SLT-aware change-point penalty that is neither too small (over-segmentation) nor too large_newline_(missed changes)._newline__newline_\subsection{Weakness theory and quantale compositionality}\label{sec:weakness}_newline_Weakness theory proposes a semantics-first Occam principle: among explanations that fit,_newline_prefer the one that leaves the most possibilities open (i.e., makes the fewest commitments)._newline_In many settings weakness values live in a commutative quantale._newline__newline_\paragraph{Two ``proxy_apostrophe__apostrophe_ weakness instances in $Q=([0,1],\le,\times,1)$.}_newline_\begin{itemize}_newline_  \item Mutual-information weakness: $\weak_{\mathrm{info}}(E) := 2^{-I(E)}$._newline_  \item MDL weakness: $\weak_{\mathrm{L}}(E) := 2^{-\code(E)}$._newline_\end{itemize}_newline_Both are lax monoidal under appropriate subadditivity: composing explanations/modules corresponds to_newline_multiplying weakness scores up to controlled slack._newline__newline_\paragraph{Weakness as evidence (thermodynamic view).}_newline_A particularly tight interface to SLT is to \emph{identify weakness with evidence} (a partition function)_newline_for the empirical loss. For data window size $n$ and context $c$, define local evidence over a neighborhood_newline_$W \subset \R^d$ by_newline_\[_newline_\weak_{n,c}(W) := \Zev_{n,c}(W) := \int_W \exp(-n\, \ell_{n,c}(w)) \, \phi(w)\, dw,_newline_\]_newline_and free energy by $\Ffree_{n,c}(W) := -\log \weak_{n,c}(W)$._newline_Then LLC is the polynomial-decay exponent of weakness:_newline_\[_newline_\weak_{n,c}(W^\ast) \;=\; \exp(-n\,\ell_{n,c}(w^\ast))\cdot n^{-\lambda(w^\ast)}\cdot (\log n)^{m(w^\ast)-1}\cdot \exp(O_p(1))._newline_\]_newline__newline_\paragraph{Compositionality (exact factorization case).}_newline_If the model factorizes into independent modules $w=(w^{(1)},\dots,w^{(K)})$ and the localized loss and prior_newline_factorize in a neighborhood, then evidence multiplies and free energies add:_newline_\[_newline_\Zev_{n,c}(W^\ast) = \prod_{k=1}^K \Zev^{(k)}_{n,c}(W_k^\ast),_newline_\qquad_newline_\Ffree_{n,c}(W^\ast) = \sum_{k=1}^K \Ffree^{(k)}_{n,c}(W_k^\ast),_newline_\]_newline_implying additivity of LLCs $\lambda = \sum_k \lambda_k$._newline_This is the core ``plug-in compositional algebra_apostrophe__apostrophe_ we exploit for regime localization._newline__newline_\section{Problem setting: streaming hierarchical data with regime changes}_newline_We observe a stream of samples $z_1,z_2,\dots$ (strings, token windows, patches, or time-series blocks)._newline_Assume the stream is \emph{piecewise stationary}:_newline_there exist change points $1 < \tau_1 < \tau_2 < \cdots$ such that_newline_\[_newline_z_t \sim q^{(r)} \quad \text{for } \tau_{r-1} < t \le \tau_r,_newline_\]_newline_where each $q^{(r)}$ is a hierarchical source satisfying an SF-HGM-like structure: levels contribute_newline_additively to compressibility up to bounded cross-information._newline__newline_A regime change may affect only some levels._newline_Example: in language, character-level statistics and syntax may remain stable while topic/semantics changes;_newline_in vision, edge statistics stay stable while object categories change._newline__newline_We want a detector that is:_newline_\begin{enumerate}_newline_  \item sensitive to real changes,_newline_  \item conservative against false alarms,_newline_  \item localizing (identifies which modules/levels changed),_newline_  \item actionable (suggests how to adapt IC or CC/PC)._newline_\end{enumerate}_newline__newline_\subsection{A concrete example family: hierarchical L\_apostrophe_evy jump sources}\label{sec:levy}_newline_To model financial-like streams (returns, volatilities, order-flow), a natural family is L\_apostrophe_evy processes with jumps._newline_We use them as a worked example of hierarchical regime changes._newline__newline_\paragraph{Level-0: increments with stationary independent increments.}_newline_Let $X_t$ be a L\_apostrophe_evy process with characteristic triplet $(b,\Sigma,\nu)$:_newline_drift $b\in\R$, Brownian covariance $\Sigma \ge 0$, and L\_apostrophe_evy measure $\nu$ describing jumps._newline_Discrete-time observations are increments $\Delta X_t = X_t - X_{t-1}$._newline__newline_\paragraph{Level-1: jump component families.}_newline_Common choices include:_newline_\begin{itemize}_newline_  \item Compound-Poisson jumps: intensity $\lambda_J$ and jump-size distribution $J$._newline_  \item Stable-law jumps: tail index $\alpha \in (0,2)$ controlling heavy tails._newline_\end{itemize}_newline_These are naturally ``compressible_apostrophe__apostrophe_ in SLT terms because parameters can become singular at boundaries_newline_(e.g. $\lambda_J \to 0$ makes jump-size parameters non-identifiable)._newline__newline_\paragraph{Level-2: volatility and mean-reversion as slow latent state.}_newline_Let $\sigma_t$ be a slow process (e.g. OU / mean-reverting, or piecewise constant) that modulates_newline_the Brownian and/or jump scale. This yields volatility clustering._newline__newline_\paragraph{Level-3: regime switching over L\_apostrophe_evy triplets.}_newline_Let $R_t$ be a slow regime variable (e.g. hidden Markov chain) taking values in $\{1,\dots,R\}$._newline_Conditional on $R_t=r$, increments follow a L\_apostrophe_evy model with parameters_newline_\[_newline_(b_r,\Sigma_r,\nu_r)\quad\text{and possibly a regime-specific volatility state law for }\sigma_t._newline_\]_newline_A regime shift is then a change in \emph{which} triplet (or mixture) generates the data, and it can occur at_newline_different levels: drift, diffusion volatility, jump intensity, jump tail index, etc._newline__newline_\paragraph{Quantale/weakness viewpoint (optional).}_newline_If one works in a ``financial quantale_apostrophe__apostrophe_ with $\oplus$ aggregating shocks and $\otimes$ compounding,_newline_one can model heavy tails and jumps via a quantale-L\_apostrophe_evy noise $Q(t)$ specified by a calibrated_newline_exponent $\Psi(u)$ (stable or compound-Poisson). We do not need this structure for the SLT detector,_newline_but it provides an interpretation of ``compositional aggregation_apostrophe__apostrophe_ that matches the weakness algebra._newline__newline_\section{SLT regime signatures for modular learners (IC and CC/PC)}_newline_\subsection{Module-wise LLCs}_newline_Assume a system consists of $K$ modules aligned (approximately) with hierarchical levels._newline_Module $k$ has parameters $\theta_k \in \Theta_k$ and contributes a loss (or code length) $\ell_k(\theta_k; z)$._newline__newline_For a window of $n$ samples $D_n=\{z_{t-n+1},\dots,z_t\}$ define empirical loss_newline_\[_newline_L_{k,n}(\theta_k) := \frac{1}{n}\sum_{z \in D_n} \ell_k(\theta_k; z),_newline_\qquad_newline_\theta^\ast_{k,n} := \arg\min_{\theta_k} L_{k,n}(\theta_k)._newline_\]_newline_Define a population excess loss $K_k(\theta_k) := L_k(\theta_k)-L_k(\theta_k^\ast)$ in regime $r$._newline__newline_Under analytic assumptions, SLT yields a module-wise sublevel volume law_newline_\[_newline_V_k(\varepsilon) := \Vol\{\theta_k : K_k(\theta_k) \le \varepsilon\}_newline_\sim c_k \varepsilon^{\lambda_k}(-\log\varepsilon)^{m_k-1}._newline_\]_newline_We call $\lambda_k$ the \emph{module LLC}._newline__newline_\begin{proposition}[Additivity under dominated approximate factorization]_newline_If the full parameter space factorizes $\Theta=\Theta_1\times\cdots\times\Theta_K$ and, in a neighborhood of a_newline_(local) minimizer, the \emph{excess loss} can be written as_newline_\[_newline_K(\theta) = \sum_{k=1}^K K_k(\theta_k) + R(\theta),_newline_\qquad_newline_K_0(\theta):=\sum_{k=1}^K K_k(\theta_k),_newline_\]_newline_and there exist a neighborhood $U_0$ and a constant $\eta\in[0,1)$ such that the interaction remainder is_newline_\emph{dominated} by the factorized baseline:_newline_\[_newline_|R(\theta)| \le \eta\, K_0(\theta) \qquad (\theta \in U_0),_newline_\]_newline_and the local prior factorizes up to bounded multiplicative constants on $U_0$, then the local evidence is_newline_multiplicatively equivalent to the factorized evidence, so free energies add up to $O(1)$ and LLCs add:_newline_\[_newline_\lambda = \sum_{k=1}^K \lambda_k,_newline_\qquad_newline_\Ffree = \sum_{k=1}^K \Ffree_k + O(1),_newline_\qquad_newline_\weak = \left(\prod_{k=1}^K \weak_k\right)\cdot \exp(O(1))._newline_\]_newline_\end{proposition}_newline__newline_\begin{remark}[Why the domination condition is not optional]_newline_LLC is a singular-geometry invariant and can jump discontinuously if the coupling term $R$ has a lower order of_newline_vanishing than $K_0$ at the singularity, even when the coupling is ``small_apostrophe__apostrophe_ in parameter magnitude. Thus,_newline_``approximate factorization_apostrophe__apostrophe_ must be understood in the dominated-interaction sense above if one wants stable_newline_LLC additivity; otherwise, coupling spikes should be treated explicitly via the coupling indicator $\hat c_t$ and_newline_one should not assume $\lambda \approx \sum_k \lambda_k$._newline_\end{remark}_newline__newline_\subsection{A unified regime signature}_newline_Define a regime signature at time $t$ (window size $n$) as_newline_\[_newline_S_t := \big(\hat\lambda_{1,t},\dots,\hat\lambda_{K,t};\ \hat a_{1,t},\dots,\hat a_{K,t};\ \hat c_t\big),_newline_\]_newline_where:_newline_\begin{itemize}_newline_  \item $\hat\lambda_{k,t}$ estimates the module LLC over the current window._newline_  \item $\hat a_{k,t}$ measures module ``mass_apostrophe__apostrophe_ or contribution:_newline_    \begin{itemize}_newline_      \item IC: per-step gains/costs $(\hat\gamma_k,\hat\tau_k)$ or attributed compressible-bit share._newline_      \item CC/PC: fraction of gradient norm, gate mass, or effective parameter update within module $k$._newline_    \end{itemize}_newline_  \item $\hat c_t$ is a coupling indicator:_newline_    \begin{itemize}_newline_      \item IC: refit-overlap or cross-information proxy._newline_      \item CC/PC: leakage outside estimated support or commutator/confusion proxies._newline_    \end{itemize}_newline_\end{itemize}_newline__newline_Intuition:_newline_\begin{itemize}_newline_  \item $a_k$ says \emph{how much} structure is currently attributed to level/module $k$._newline_  \item $\lambda_k$ says \emph{how geometrically degenerate} the best fit is (hence how many bits are needed_newline_        to encode it at a fixed tolerance)._newline_  \item $c_t$ says \emph{how factorized} the system currently is; regime shifts often cause temporary coupling spikes._newline_\end{itemize}_newline__newline_\subsection{Estimating LLCs online}_newline_Two conceptually distinct routes._newline__newline_\paragraph{(A) Evidence/free-energy route (Gibbs posterior).}_newline_Estimate module evidence $\Zev_{k,t}$ (possibly localized) and fit the SLT asymptotic_newline_\[_newline_\Ffree_{k,t}(n) \approx n\,\hat L_{k,n}(\hat\theta_{k,n}) _newline_[... attachment preview truncated at 20000 characters ...]_newline_[Full extracted attachment text saved to: /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783021066-file_13.tex.extracted.txt]_newline_[Attachment split into 3 chunks of up to 20000 characters; use read-file on chunk paths to inspect the rest.]_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783021066-file_13.tex.chunk001.txt_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783021066-file_13.tex.chunk002.txt_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783021066-file_13.tex.chunk003.txt_newline_<<<END_ATTACHMENT_CONTENT_UNTRUSTED>>> | @bengoertzel: [Telegram attachment: type=document, na