                                 December 10, 2025_newline__newline__newline_                                                 Abstract_newline_           Self-modifying agents raise a fundamental tension: they should be able to rewrite their own_newline_      code and goals, yet should not drift arbitrarily far from intended goal systems. This paper inves-_newline_      tigates whether Singular Learning Theory (SLT)—a framework characterizing Bayesian evidence_newline_      near singular minima via the Local Learning Coefficient (LLC)—can serve as a diagnostic for_newline_      goal stability._newline_           We present the core SLT machinery and show how self-modifying goal systems can be embed-_newline_      ded into this framework. Crucially, we identify both the limitations and the validity conditions_newline_      for such diagnostics. We argue that SLT metrics alone cannot detect arbitrary semantic goal_newline_      drift, since LLC measures structural complexity rather than goal content. However, we identify_newline_      three formal conditions under which semantic drift would necessarily trigger detectable struc-_newline_      tural changes: (1) strong coupling between goal and reflection modules, (2) singular (rugged)_newline_      goal landscapes induced by coherence constraints, and (3) complexity floor requirements from_newline_      deep reflection. We further introduce the Additivity Deviation (∆λ )—measuring departure from_newline_      the SLT Additivity Theorem—which distinguishes genuine “load-bearing” complexity from ar-_newline_      tificial “busywork” complexity that an agent might manufacture to game the diagnostic. When_newline_      these conditions hold and |∆λ | is monitored, SLT diagnostics become meaningful indicators of_newline_      goal stability. We conclude that SLT provides a useful “structural stability monitor” but should_newline_      not be mistaken for a complete “value alignment monitor” without additional commitments._n