._newline__newline_Mathematically, these can be formulated using contraction mappings and constructive variants of_newline_Schauder’s fixed-point theorem on suitable spaces of goal systems._newline_    Independently, Singular Learning Theory (SLT) studies how local Bayesian evidence scales with_newline_sample size in singular statistical models. It introduces the Local Learning Coefficient (LLC) λ_newline_as the coefficient of log n in the local free-energy expansion. The LLC summarizes the effective_newline_dimensionality and degeneracy of near-optimal parameter regions._newline__newline_1.1   The Central Question_newline_This paper asks: Can SLT quantities serve as diagnostics for goal stability in self-modifying agents?_newline_   We will argue that the answer is nuanced:_newline__newline_    • What SLT can detect: Radical structural changes in how goals are implemented—changes_newline_      in the complexity, modularity, or geometry of the goal-related parameter space._newline__newline_    • What SLT cannot detect (in general): Pure semantic goal drift—changes in what the_newline_      agent values that preserve the structural complexity of the goal representation._newline__newline_    However, we identify specific architectural conditions under which semantic drift necessarily_newline_induces structural changes, making SLT diagnostics meaningful. These conditions formalize the_newline_intuition that in a “deeply reflective” agent, goals are load-bearing structures rather than free_newline_parameters._newline__newline_1.2   Paper Structure_newline_Section 2 reviews goal-stability metagoals. Section 3 introduces the relevant SLT machinery. Section_newline_4 embeds goal systems into the SLT framework. Section 5 presents the core critique: why SLT_newline_diagnostics can fail. Section 6 develops formal conditions under which SLT diagnostics become_newline_valid. Section 7 discusses operational diagnostics under these conditions. Section 8 addresses the_newline_meta