__newline_2     Conceptual Overview_newline_2.1   The distinction-symmetry-singularity chain_newline_The mechanism linking semantics to geometry proceeds in steps:_newline_    Step 1: Semantics as distinctions. A semantic state determines_newline_which situations the agent treats as equivalent (same goal-relevant features)_newline_vs. different. A paperclip maximizer distinguishes by paperclip count; a_newline_welfare maximizer distinguishes by wellbeing indicators._newline_    Step 2: Distinctions induce partitions. Given a distinction pattern,_newline_we obtain a partition: items treated as equivalent fall in the same block._newline_Coarser distinctions yield coarser partitions (fewer, larger blocks)._newline_    Step 3: Partitions determine symmetries. The automorphism_newline_group Aut(Π) consists of permutations preserving block membership. Coarser_newline_partitions have larger automorphism groups: merging blocks of sizes j and_newline_k replaces Sym(j) × Sym(k) with Sym(j + k)._newline_    Step 4: Symmetries cause singularities. In learning, symmetries_newline_manifest as non-identifiabilities: multiple parameter settings achieving the_newline_same loss. This creates flat directions, non-isolated minima, and degenerate_newline_Fisher information—precisely what SLT’s singularity analysis captures._newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                     4_newline_2.2   A toy example: the full chain in action_newline_Before proceeding to formal development, we illustrate the complete chain_newline_with a minimal example._newline_Example 2.1 (Two-hidden-unit network). Consider a neural network f (x; w1 , w2 ) =_newline_σ(w1 x) + σ(w2 x) with two hidden units and weights w1 , w2 ∈ R._newline_    Semantic state: Suppose the agent’s “semantics” are defined by which_newline_input regions it distinguishes. If the agent treats inputs symmetrically (no_newline_distinction between “w1 -relevant” and “w2 -relevant” structure), the two hid-_newlin