newline_         SubRep is a framework for compositional goal pursuit in which options export expectation_newline_     models (r̂, n̂) and are admitted into a planner-facing library via cone-dominant (CDS) or Pareto-_newline_     dominant (PDS) tests over a motive geometry. This paper develops a rigorous singular learning_newline_     theory (SLT) analysis of SubRep systems, addressing the question: how do the local learning_newline_     coefficients (LLCs) of a goal-level system relate to those of its subgoal modules?_newline_         We extend previous work in six directions. First, we introduce explicit functional coupling_newline_     terms arising from planner–option composition and derive sufficient conditions under which such_newline_     coupling remains dominated, preserving approximate LLC additivity. Second, we analyze shared_newline_     representation architectures where options and planners share feature extractors, showing how_newline_     the shared block’s LLC contributes once (not per-module) to the total complexity. Third, we_newline_     address the non-analyticity introduced by hard CDS/PDS admission tests by introducing soft_newline_     relaxations and proving convergence of the relaxed LLC to a well-defined stratified limit. Fourth,_newline_     we develop a comprehensive treatment of non-convex motive geometries, decomposing general_newline_     motive spaces into convex cells, analyzing the tropical/piecewise-linear structure that emerges,_newline_     and characterizing singularities at cell boundaries via a double stratification. Fifth, we analyze_newline_     the robustness of additivity, proving quantitative bounds on the “additivity gap” as conditions_newline_     degrade, and characterizing graceful versus catastrophic failure modes. Sixth, and most sig-_newline_     nificantly, we move beyond additivity by introducing LLC interaction complexity—an analog of_newline_     mutual information that captures how subgoal complexities combin