totext>>>_newline_                           Weakness as Local Evidence:_newline_                       A Systems Theory for Degeneracy,_newline_                 Refinement DAGs, and Continual Adaptation,_newline_                      with Transformers as a Case Study_newline_                                               Ben Goertzel_newline__newline_                                            December 6, 2025_newline__newline__newline_                                                  Abstract_newline_          We propose a systems-level formalism for continual learning in nonstationary environments_newline_      that unifies three themes: (1) the local-learning-coefficient (LLC) view of degeneracy from singular_newline_      learning theory (SLT); (2) “weakness” as an Occam-like bias formalized in thermodynamic_newline_      quantales; and (3) causal-coding style modularization that makes continual learning robust by_newline_      enforcing approximate commutativity of context-conditioned updates._newline_          The core technical insight is that it can be effective to define weakness to be the (local)_newline_      Bayesian evidence/volume object that SLT already analyzes. Under this definition, the LLC is_newline_      literally the asymptotic invariant of weakness in the large-sample (or low-temperature) limit. We_newline_      then show how tropicalization (log-sum-exp dominance) yields a min-plus semiring in which MAP_newline_      selection over a DAG of candidate factorizations becomes shortest-path dynamic programming._newline_      This yields a clean mathematical model of “updating many approximate factorizations per_newline_      context”: quantale-message-passing over a refinement DAG._newline_          We apply the framework to (i) stagewise degeneracy changes observed during standard_newline_      backprop Transformer training, interpreted as posterior switching between neighborhoods with_newline_      different LLCs, and to (ii) predictive/causal