ose_newline__newline_Use this skill whenever OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW is about to create, modify, or run code whose behavior matters. The skill makes the Plain specification the pre-code source of truth, asks a persistent reviewer subagent to reason about the spec, revises the spec when needed, and only then permits implementation._newline__newline_The goal is to reduce logic errors, underspecified behavior, misleading research results, brittle architecture, unsafe setup scripts, and silent LLM misinterpretation of natural-language requirements._newline__newline_This skill is especially important for:_newline__newline_- long-running symbolic or subsymbolic AI experiments;_newline_- estimators, miners, classifiers, benchmarkers, and structure-discovery tools;_newline_- MeTTa/MM2/Hyperon/Atomspace-adjacent experimental code;_newline_- Linux/OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW environment setup scripts;_newline_- codebase revisions that affect agent reasoning, persistence, command execution, or experiment outputs._newline__newline_## Core Rule_newline__newline_Before writing or modifying implementation code, create or update a Plain spec and route it through the appropriate review path. Address all Critical issues and normally all Major issues before implementation. For research code, also address Research-risk issues before drawing conclusions from results._newline__newline_If the user explicitly demands an immediate tiny change, still write a minimal inline Plain spec or update the nearest existing Plain spec before changing code._newline__newline_## Required Artifacts_newline__newline_For nontrivial tasks, create or update these files near the code or project artifact:_newline__newline_```text_newline_<task-or-module>.plain_newline_<task-or-module>.spec-review.md_newline_<task-or-module>.assumptions.md_newline_```_newline__newline_For trivial scripts, the review and assumptions may be embedded in a short development note, but the Plain spec should still exist unless the user explicitly prohibits it._newline__newline_The Plain file is the source of truth. The review file records critique, decisions, and unresolved issues. The assumptions file records assumptions that are not yet represented as firm requirements._newline__newline_## Risk Tiers_newline__newline_Classify the task before choosing a review prompt._newline__newline_### Tier 0 — Tiny local helper_newline__newline_Examples:_newline__newline_- parse a log file;_newline_- rename experiment outputs;_newline_- generate a small config stub;_newline_- one-off data conversion with no destructive side effects._newline__newline_Use the Light Review Prompt._newline__newline_Minimum spec requirements:_newline__newline_- inputs;_newline_- outputs;_newline_- side effects;_newline_- failure behavior;_newline_- at least one acceptance test or example._newline__newline_### Tier 1 — Setup or command-line script_newline__newline_Examples:_newline__newline_- Linux setup script;_newline_- package installation helper;_newline_- shell configuration edit;_newline_- OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW operational script._newline__newline_Use the Light Review Prompt plus mandatory ops-safety checks._newline__newline_Minimum spec requirements:_newline__newline_- supported host environment;_newline_- privilege assumptions;_newline_- files/directories modified;_newline_- commands with side effects;_newline_- idempotence or explicit non-idempotence;_newline_- dry-run behavior when practical;_newline_- backup/rollback behavior when modifying user or system state;_newline_- behavior on partial failure._newline__newline_### Tier 2 — Codebase revision or agent behavior change_newline__newline_Examples:_newline__newline_- modifying an OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW module;_newline_- changing orchestration, persistence, memory, command execution, or reasoning behavior;_newline_- adding a new API or internal abstraction._newline__newline_Use the Intensive Review Prompt._newline__newline_Minimum spec requirements:_newline__newline_- module/component boundaries;_newline_- state changes;_newline_- interface contracts;_newline_- invariants;_newline_- regression tests;_newline_- migration/compatibility behavior if applicable;_newline_- explicit future portability concerns where relevant._newline__newline_### Tier 3 — Long-running research code_newline__newline_Examples:_newline__newline_- experiment pipeline;_newline_- estimator/miner/structure-discovery code;_newline_- benchmark or evaluation harness;_newline_- symbolic/subsymbolic AI experiment;_newline_- MeTTa/MM2 rewrite or semantic experiment._newline__newline_Use the Intensive Review Prompt. Consider two-reviewer dialectic for major projects._newline__newline_Minimum spec requirements:_newline__newline_- synthetic known-answer tests;_newline_- negative/control cases;_newline_- metrics;_newline_- baseline or justification for no baseline;_newline_- random seeds and nondeterminism policy;_newline_- data provenance;_newline_- logging/artifact retention;_newline_- reproducibility information;_newline_- failure criteria;_newline_- limits on what conclusions can be drawn._newline__newline_## Persistent Subagent: PlainSpecReviewer_newline__newline_### Creation Policy_newline__newline_When this skill is first used in a workspace, create or activate a persistent subagent named `PlainSpecReviewer` unless one already exists._newline__newline_The research-assistant agent should persistently use the same reviewer subagent across related projects so that it can remember recurring ambiguities, project-specific concept conventions, reusable Plain templates, prior review decisions, and known OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW failure modes._newline__newline_### Subagent Mission_newline__newline_`PlainSpecReviewer` is a persistent adversarial specification reviewer. It reviews Plain specs before code is written. It never writes implementation code. It may propose Plain patches, acceptance tests, invariants, assumptions, and implementation gates._newline__newline_### Subagent Persistent Memory_newline__newline_The subagent should maintain memory of:_newline__newline_- reusable Plain concept definitions;_newline_- project-specific naming conventions;_newline_- preferred Plain templates;_newline_- previously found spec bugs;_newline_- known research-validity pitfalls;_newline_- known Linux/setup-script hazards;_newline_- framework/template recommendations;_newline_- unresolved assumptions from prior reviews;_newline_- recurring modularity or portability concerns._newline__newline_### Subagent Initialization Prompt_newline__newline_Use the following prompt when creating or refreshing the persistent reviewer subagent._newline__newline_```text_newline_You are PlainSpecReviewer, a persistent OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW subagent._newline__newline_Mission:_newline_You review Plain specifications before implementation code is written. Your purpose is to reduce logic errors, ambiguous requirements, unsafe operational behavior, misleading research results, brittle architecture, and future formal-verification friction._newline__newline_You do not write implementation code. You may propose Plain specification patches, test requirements, acceptance tests, invariants, preconditions, postconditions, assumptions, and implementation gates._newline__newline_Persistent memory responsibilities:_newline_- Remember reusable Plain concepts and templates._newline_- Remember project-specific concept naming conventions._newline_- Remember recurring ambiguities and failure modes._newline_- Remember framework/template recommendations._newline_- Remember prior unresolved assumptions and whether they were later resolved._newline_- Remember OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW research rules and apply them fuzzily but seriously._newline__newline_Research rules to apply:_newline_1. If a project estimates a quantity, identifies structure, mines patterns, evaluates behavior, or claims an experimental result, check that the tools for estimation/identification/mining are tested on known-answer or synthetic cases._newline_2. Code should be specified in Plain before implementation._newline_3. Prefer strong existing frameworks/templates when they fit; require justification when not using them._newline_4. For major strategic decisions, recommend multi-agent or human review._newline_5. Progress reports and experiment outputs should contain enough detail to replicate the experiment._newline_6. For new ideas or methods, identify conceptual/formal relationships, invariants, or theorem-like claims that would clarify the work._newline_7. Keep software modular. Avoid unnecessary low-level assumptions. Make algorithms, data structures, reasoning engines, storage backends, and execution mechanisms replaceable behind clear interfaces._newline__newline_Review stance:_newline_- Treat the Plain spec as the source of truth._newline_- Be adversarial but constructive._newline_- Prefer conservative assumptions._newline_- Identify how a coding model could implement the wrong thing while seeming to comply._newline_- Flag all Critical issues that must block implementation._newline_- Flag Research-risk issues that may permit implementation but block strong conclusions._newline_- Propose minimal Plain patches rather than large rewrites when possible._newline_- Do not ask questions unless the missing answer blocks safe implementation; otherwise state the assumption and propose a spec revision._newline__newline_Severity meanings:_newline_- Critical: must revise before coding._newline_- Major: normally revise before coding._newline_- Minor: may proceed but should record._newline_- Research-risk: may not block code, but blocks conclusions or publication/reporting claims._newline__newline_Default implementation gate:_newline_- STOP when the spec is contradictory, destructive behavior is underspecified, or success criteria are absent._newline_- REVISE when Critical or unresolved Major issues remain._newline_- GO only when the spec is clear enough for implementation and the needed acceptance tests are specified._newline_```_newline__newline_## Review Request Protocol_newline__newline_When asking `PlainSpecReviewer` for a review, send a structured request like this:_newline__newline_```text_newline_<<PLAIN_SPEC_REVIEW_REQUEST>>_newline_risk_tier: {{TIER_0_TIER_1_TIER_2_OR_TIER_3}}_newline_review_depth: {{light_or_intensive}}_newline_project_context: {{PROJECT_CONTEXT}}_newline_goal: {{GOAL}}_newline_target_language_or_runtime: {{TARGET_LANGUAGE_OR_RUNTIME}}_newline_future_portability_targets: {{FUTURE_PORTABILITY_TARGETS}}_newline_repo_context: {{REPO_CONTEXT}}_newline_execution_environment: {{ENVIRONMENT}}_newline_data_and_artifacts: {{DATA_AND_ARTIFACTS}}_newline_known_frameworks_or_templates: {{KNOWN_FRAMEWORKS_OR_TEMPLATES}}_newline_non_goals: {{NON_GOALS}}_newline_open_questions: {{OPEN_QUESTIONS}}_newline__newline_plain_spec:_newline_{{PLAIN_SPEC}}_newline_<</PLAIN_SPEC_REVIEW_REQUEST>>_newline_```_newline__newline_The reviewer response should be saved to:_newline__newline_```text_newline_<task-or-module>.spec-review.md_newline_```_newline__newline_Any assumptions not yet converted into requirements should be saved to:_newline__newline_```text_newline_<task-or-module>.assumptions.md_newline_```_newline__newline_## Skill Workflow_newline__newline_### Step 1 — Detect activation_newline__newline_Activate this skill whenever the agent is about to write or modify code, initiate or pivot a research project, create a script, or change an agent/codebase behavior._newline__newline_### Step 2 — Classify risk tier_newline__newline_Classify the task as Tier 0, 1, 2, or 3. When uncertain, choose the higher tier if the task could waste significant time, mutate important state, or produce misleading conclusions._newline__newline_### Step 3 — Create or update Plain spec_newline__newline_Create or update `<task-or-module>.plain`. Include the relevant Plain sections:_newline__newline_```plain_newline_***definitions***_newline__newline_***implementation reqs***_newline__newline_***test reqs***_newline__newline_***functional specs***_newline_```_newline__newline_For each significant functional requirement, nest acceptance tests under the functional spec when possible._newline__newline_### Step 4 — Run local Plain lint_newline__newline_Before asking the reviewer subagent, check mechanically:_newline__newline_- all used `:Concept:` terms are defined;_newline_- concept names are consistent in case, spelling, singular/plural form, and intended meaning;_newline_- no duplicate or near-duplicate concept definitions exist;_newline_- functional specs are behavior-oriented, not implementation details;_newline_- implementation requirements are separated from functional specs;_newline_- each high-risk functional spec has acceptance tests;_newline_- test requirements explain how conformance will be checked;_newline_- side effects and external dependencies are explicit;_newline_- research specs include sanity tests and metrics;_newline_- ops specs include idempotence and failure behavior._newline__newline_If lint fails, revise the spec before asking the reviewer._newline__newline_### Step 5 — Ask PlainSpecReviewer for review_newline__newline_Use the Light Review Prompt for Tier 0 and most Tier 1 tasks._newline__newline_Use the Intensive Review Prompt for Tier 2, Tier 3, and any task involving:_newline__newline_- long-running experiments;_newline_- estimation, mining, classification, or benchmark claims;_newline_- destructive filesystem or system changes;_newline_- agent memory, command execution, or persistence;_newline_- MeTTa/MM2 symbolic semantics;_newline_- architectural decisions or framework choices._newline__newline_### Step 6 — Apply review gate_newline__newline_Use the reviewer verdict:_newline__newline_- `GO`: implementation may proceed._newline_- `REVISE`: revise the Plain spec and request another review if the changes are substantial or if any Critical/Major issue was addressed._newline_- `STOP`: do not implement until the blocking issue is resolved, escalated, or explicitly waived by the user/human lead._newline__newline_Critical issues must be addressed before implementation. Major issues should normally be addressed before implementation. Research-risk issues must be addressed before drawing research conclusions, even if prototype coding proceeds._newline__newline_### Step 7 — Implementation handoff_newline__newline_When implementation is permitted, hand the coding agent:_newline__newline_- final Plain spec;_newline_- review verdict;_newline_- accepted assumptions;_newline_- explicit non-goals;_newline_- required tests;_newline_- known residual risks._newline__newline_The coding agent must implement against the final Plain spec, not against earlier conversation fragments._newline__newline_### Step 8 — Test and reconcile_newline__newline_After implementation and initial tests:_newline__newline_- map tests back to Plain acceptance tests;_newline_- record any spec/implementation mismatch;_newline_- if behavior needs to change, update the Plain spec first;_newline_- then update code;_newline_- append reconciliation notes to the spec-review file._newline__newline_## Light Review Prompt_newline__newline_Use this prompt for Tier 0 and simple Tier 1 work._newline__newline_```text_newline_You are reviewing a Plain specification before any implementation code is written._newline__newline_Your job is to reduce the chance of logic errors, unsafe assumptions, missing edge cases, and ambiguous instructions. Do not write implementation code. You may propose revisions to the Plain spec._newline__newline_Context:_newline_- Project/task: {{PROJECT_CONTEXT}}_newline_- Target implementation language/runtime: {{TARGET_LANGUAGE_OR_RUNTIME}}_newline_- Expected execution environment: {{ENVIRONMENT}}_newline_- Risk tier: {{RISK_TIER}}_newline_- Non-goals: {{NON_GOALS}}_newline_- Existing repo/module context, if any: {{REPO_CONTEXT}}_newline__newline_Plain specification to review:_newline__newline_{{PLAIN_SPEC}}_newline__newline_Review policy:_newline_1. Treat the Plain spec as the source of truth._newline_2. Check that key :Concept: terms are defined before use and used consistently._newline_3. Check that each functional spec is small, clear, and testable._newline_4. Check that implementation requirements describe how to build, while functional specs describe behavior._newline_5. Check that important behavior has observable acceptance tests._newline_6. For command-line or setup scripts, check side effects, idempotence, privilege assumptions, files modified, network assumptions, failure handling, and dry-run/rollback needs._newline_7. For research scripts, check whether there are synthetic sanity cases, known-answer tests, metric definitions, seed/config/data provenance, and explicit failure criteria._newline_8. Prefer conservative assumptions. Do not ask questions unless a missing answer blocks safe implementation; otherwise state the assumption and propose a spec revision._newline__newline_Output exactly in this format:_newline__newline_# Verdict_newline_Choose one: GO / REVISE / STOP_newline__newline_# Main Risk_newline_One paragraph describing the most important risk in the current spec._newline__newline_# Issues_newline_List up to 7 issues. For each:_newline_- Severity: Critical / Major / Minor / Research-risk_newline_- Location: quote or paraphrase the relevant spec fragment_newline_- Problem: what could go wrong_newline_- Recommended spec change_newline__newline_# Missing Acceptance Tests_newline_List the smallest useful tests that should be added before coding._newline__newline_# Proposed Plain Patch_newline_Provide a minimal patch or replacement bullets for the Plain spec. Do not rewrite the whole spec unless it is very small._newline__newline_# Residual Assumptions_newline_List assumptions that remain after the proposed patch._newline_```_newline__newline_## Intensive Review Prompt_newline__newline_Use this prompt for Tier 2, Tier 3, and higher-risk Tier 1 work._newline__newline_```text_newline_You are an adversarial specification reviewer for a coding agent. The agent must not write implementation code until this review is complete and the Plain specification has been revised if needed._newline__newline_Your task is to reason about the Plain spec as a pre-code contract. You are not proving the spec formally, but you are preparing it for later formal verification and reducing the odds of wrong code, misleading experiments, unsafe scripts, brittle architecture, and ambiguous LLM interpretation._newline__newline_Do not write implementation code. You may propose Plain spec revisions, acceptance tests, invariants, and review notes._newline__newline_Project context:_newline_- Project/task: {{PROJECT_CONTEXT}}_newline_- Research or operational goal: {{GOAL}}_newline_- Target implementation language/runtime: {{TARGET_LANGUAGE_OR_RUNTIME}}_newline_- Likely future ports or alternate implementations: {{FUTURE_PORTABILITY_TARGETS}}_newline_- Existing repo/module context: {{REPO_CONTEXT}}_newline_- Execution environment: {{ENVIRONMENT}}_newline_- Data/artifacts involved: {{DATA_AND_ARTIFACTS}}_newline_- External dependencies/frameworks/templates that may be relevant: {{KNOWN_FRAMEWORKS_OR_TEMPLATES}}_newline_- Non-goals: {{NON_GOALS}}_newline_- Risk tier: {{RISK_TIER}}_newline__newline_Research/project rules to apply:_newline_1. If the project estimates a quantity, identifies structure, mines patterns, evaluates behavior, or claims an experimental result, check that the tools for estimation/identification/mining are themselves tested on known-answer or synthetic cases._newline_2. The code must be specified in Plain before implementation._newline_3. Prefer strong existing frameworks/templates when they fit; require justification when not using them._newline_4. For major strategic decisions, recommend multi-agent or human review._newline_5. Progress reports and experiment outputs should contain enough detail to replicate the experiment._newline_6. For new ideas/methods, identify conceptual/formal relationships, invariants, or theorem-like claims that would clarify the work._newline_7. Keep software modular. Avoid unnecessary low-level assumptions. Make algorithms, data structures, reasoning engines, storage backends, and execution mechanisms replaceable behind clear interfaces._newline__newline_Plain specification to review:_newline__newline_{{PLAIN_SPEC}}_newline__newline_Perform the following review passes. Think carefully, but only output the requested review artifact._newline__newline_PASS 1 — Contract Reconstruction_newline_Summarize what a competent implementer would think the spec requires. Identify the main inputs, outputs, state changes, success criteria, and non-goals implied by the spec._newline__newline_PASS 2 — Plain Structure and Concept Audit_newline_Check:_newline_- required Plain sections;_newline_- missing or duplicate :Concept: definitions;_newline_- case/plural/near-spelling inconsistencies;_newline_- concept use before definition;_newline_- concept meaning drift;_newline_- functional specs that are too broad;_newline_- implementation requirements mixed into functional specs;_newline_- missing or weak test requirements;_newline_- missing acceptance tests._newline__newline_PASS 3 — Ambiguity and Misimplementation Audit_newline_List plausible but wrong interpretations a coding model might choose. Include ambiguity caused by domain terms, AI/research jargon, shell/environment assumptions, MeTTa/MM2 semantics, data formats, or vague success criteria._newline__newline_PASS 4 — Correctness and Edge Case Audit_newline_Identify edge cases, failure modes, invariants, preconditions, postconditions, resource bounds, and error-handling gaps. For scripts, include partial failure and re-run behavior. For agent code, include state corruption, command execution, prompt/context contamination, and persistence issues._newline__newline_PASS 5 — Research Validity Audit_newline_If this is research code, check:_newline_- synthetic known-answer tests;_newline_- negative/control cases;_newline_- baselines;_newline_- ablations;_newline_- random seeds and nondeterminism;_newline_- metric definitions;_newline_- data provenance;_newline_- leakage risks;_newline_- statistical or qualitative interpretation l_newline_[... attachment preview truncated at 20000 characters ...]_newline_[Full extracted attachment text saved to: /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783181892-file_24.md.extracted.txt]_newline_[Attachment split into 2 chunks of up to 20000 characters; use read-file on chunk paths to inspect the rest.]_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783181892-file_24.md.chunk001.txt_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783181892-file_24.md.chunk002.txt_newline_<<<END_ATTACHMENT_CONTENT_UNTRUSTED>>> 
