 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .            10_newline_   12.6 Failure Mode: The Spec Becomes Stale . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          10_newline__newline_13 How a Human Should Read the Skill File                                                               10_newline__newline_14 Recommended Future Extensions                                                                        11_newline_   14.1 Machine-Checkable Plain Lint . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      11_newline_   14.2 A Project-Specific Plain Concept Library . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .        11_newline_   14.3 Spec-to-Test Traceability . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .     11_newline_   14.4 Plain-to-Formalization Hooks . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .      11_newline_   14.5 Review Metrics . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .    11_newline__newline_A Appendix A: Persistent Reviewer Initialization Prompt                                                 11_newline__newline_B Appendix B: Structured Review Request Protocol                                                        13_newline__newline_C Appendix C: Light Review Prompt                                                                       13_newline__newline_D Appendix D: Intensive Review Prompt                                                                   14_newline__newline_E Appendix E: Two-Reviewer Dialectic Prompt                                                             17_newline__newline_F Appendix F: Implementation Handoff Prompt                                                             18_newline__newline_G Appendix G: Post-Implementation Reconciliation Prompt                                                 19_newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                    2_newline_H Appendix H: Minimal Plain Templates                                                              19_newline_  H.1 Python Research Script Template . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .    19_newline_  H.2 Linux/OpenCLAW Setup Script Template . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         20_newline_  H.3 MeTTa/MM2 Symbolic Experiment Template . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           21_newline_  H.4 Agent Codebase Revision Template . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .     22_newline__newline__newline_1    Purpose of the Skill_newline_The skill file exists to make a coding agent pause before implementation and ask: what exactly is_newline_the code supposed to do, how will we know whether it did it, and what mistakes might a plausible_newline_implementation make while still seeming to satisfy the request?_newline_    The target use case is OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW research and operations work, including:_newline_    • Python research code for symbolic or subsymbolic AI experiments;_newline_    • MeTTa, MM2, or related symbolic-language experiments;_newline_    • Linux command-line scripts for environment setup or agent operation;_newline_    • revisions to OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW agent codebases;_newline_    • future UX/UI code where behavior matters._newline_    The immediate motivation is pragmatic. Formal verification may eventually be used on Plain_newline_specifications or on artifacts rendered from them. But long before the formal pipeline is mature, a_newline_significant fraction of failures can be found by asking a strong LLM to reason adversarially about_newline_the spec. The skill therefore creates a governed pre-code review loop: first write or update a_newline_Plain spec, then have a persistent reviewer inspect it, then revise the spec, and only then allow_newline_implementation._newline_    The skill is intentionally not a heavyweight process for every trivial edit. It contains a light_newline_path for small scripts and a more intensive path for research code and architectural changes. The_newline_core idea is to match review cost to risk._newline__newline__newline_2    Why Plain Is the Right Boundary_newline_Plain is useful here because it gives the agent a structured, textual requirements artifact rather_newline_than a loose chat instruction. The Plain language guide describes Plain as a specification lan-_newline_guage for writing software requirements in a clear, structured format. Its standard sections in-_newline_clude ***definitions***, ***implementation reqs***, ***test reqs***, and ***functional_newline_specs***. It also supports nested ***acceptance tests*** under functional specs. See the Plain_newline_documentation at https://plainlang.org/docs/language-guide/basic-syntax/._newline_    For an agentic coding workflow, this matters for several reasons._newline_    First, Plain separates roles that are often blurred in chat-based coding. The definitions_newline_section records the domain vocabulary. The implementation reqs section says how the code_newline_should be constrained or structured. The test reqs section says how conformance tests should be_newline_built or executed. The functional specs section says what behavior the software must implement._newline_This separation gives both the coding agent and the reviewer subagent better handles for detecting_newline_errors._newline_    Second, Plain’s :Concept: notation makes ambiguous terms inspectable. A Plain reviewer_newline_can check whether a concept has been defined, whether it is used before definition, whether near-_newline_duplicates exist, and whether the same concept appears to drift in meaning._newline_    Third, Plain functional specs are expected to be small and testable. The Plain docs advise_newline_that each functional-spec bullet should describe a single piece of functionality, use defined concepts_newline__newline__newline_                                                  3_newline_consistently, and remain clear, testable, and limited in complexity. See https://plainlang.org/_newline_docs/language-guide/functional-specs/. This fits well with LLM review: the reviewer can_newline_attack each bullet for ambiguity, edge cases, and missing acceptance tests._newline_    Fourth, Plain source is plain text. It can be committed with code, diffed, reviewed, patched,_newline_and used as an implementation handoff artifact. This makes it suitable not only for future formal_newline_verification, but also for current human and multi-agent workflows._newline__newline__newline_3    The Main Design Principle: A Specification Gate_newline_The skill treats the Plain spec as a pre-code contract. The coding agent should not write imple-_newline_mentation code until the relevant Plain spec has passed a review gate or has received an explicit_newline_human waiver._newline_    The gate is not meant to prove the spec correct. It is meant to reduce avoidable mistakes before_newline_they become more expensive. The reviewer asks questions such as:_newline_    • Are the main concepts defined and used consistently?_newline_    • Are the functional requirements small, observable, and testable?_newline_    • Are implementation constraints separated from behavior?_newline_    • Could a coding model implement the wrong thing while appearing compliant?_newline_    • Are edge cases and failure modes specified?_newline_    • For scripts, are side effects, idempotence, privilege assumptions, and partial failures specified?_newline_    • For research code, are known-answer tests, metrics, baselines, seeds, and artifact-retention_newline_      rules specified?_newline_    • For agent code, are state changes, external actions, safety boundaries, and regression cases_newline_      specified?_newline_    The result is a verdict: GO, REVISE, or STOP. A GO verdict permits implementation. A REVISE_newline_verdict requires spec changes before implementation, unless the remaining issues are explicitly_newline_accepted as non-blocking. A STOP verdict blocks implementation until the underlying problem is_newline_resolved or escalated._newline__newline__newline_4    Why Use a Persistent Reviewer Subagent?_newline_A one-off review prompt is useful, but a persistent subagent is more useful for a long-lived research_newline_environment. The skill therefore creates or activates a subagent named PlainSpecReviewer._newline_   The reviewer is persistent so that it can remember recurring project-specific patterns, including:_newline_   • reusable Plain concept definitions;_newline_   • naming conventions for OpenCLAW/OmegaCLAW specs;_newline_   • prior ambiguity patterns;_newline_   • common Linux setup hazards;_newline_   • recurring research-validity failures;_newline_   • preferred frameworks and templates;_newline_   • unresolved assumptions from prior reviews;_newline_   • past cases where a coding model implemented the wrong thing._newline_   This persistence turns the reviewer from a generic critic into a project memory component. Over_newline_time, it should develop useful local taste: which concepts are usually meant by :AgentState:, what_newline_counts as an :Artifact: in a particular lab workflow, which setup-script files are dangerous to_newline_overwrite, and which research methods have previously needed synthetic sanity cases._newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                   4_newline_   The reviewer subagent is not allowed to write implementation code. This restriction is impor-_newline_tant. Its job is to preserve the distinction between specification reasoning and implementation. It_newline_may propose Plain patches, acceptance tests, invariants, assumptions, and implementation gates,_newline_but it should not start solving the programming task directly._newline__newline__newline_5    Risk Tiers_newline_The skill uses risk tiers because not all coding tasks deserve the same amount of ceremony. The_newline_following table summarizes the intended tiers._new