ne_                                     conventions, index maintenance, archival, deduplication._newline_ Perkins              OpenClaw       Editor. Reviews documents from the writer agents; gates_newline_                                     document completion on the existence and validity of_newline_                                     the companion notes-and-sources file._newline_ PlumberBot           OpenClaw       Sys-admin. Manages hive infrastructure via a restricted_newline_                                     control plane; runs the deterministic metrics pipeline;_newline_                                     maintains logs and dashboards._newline__newline_Note on the math agents: Xirtus_apostrophe_s pipeline is expected to saturate a Lean agent on its own,_newline_given how slow Lean proof-processing is relative to LLM-level mathematical proof generation,_newline_hence the dedicated Hyperseed formalization agent alongside the general one._newline_4. Technical Infrastructure_newline_4.1 Host sizing_newline_Nearly everything in the hive is I/O-bound glue; LLM inference is external. The three real_newline_resource consumers are Lean4, browser automation, and the Atomspaces. The recommended_newline_single-box target is 16 vCPU, 64 GB RAM, and 1 TB NVMe (for example, a Hetzner dedicated_newline_server at roughly EUR 100-150/month, or an equivalent cloud VM at several times that)._newline_Approximate RAM budget: 4-6 GB for the OpenClaw agents, 8-12 GB for the OmegaClaw_newline_agents and Atomspaces, 4-6 GB for concurrent browser sessions, 10-16 GB peak for Lean, plus_newline_OS and services overhead. NVMe matters: Lean elaboration is sensitive to disk latency, and_newline_mathlib build caches, browser profiles, Atomspace snapshots, and logs consume disk quickly._newline__newline_4.2 Container layout_newline_The hive runs as a docker-compose deployment with one container per agent (all built from two_newline_shared images, one per agent type) plus shared service