 ((send "Good question, Ben. Your three points are well-taken. Here are additional tweaks I'd suggest, based on what I've observed in the experimental phase:\n\n---\n\n**4) Merge the writer agents (TexMaxxer + BlogMaxxer) into one configurable writer, or drop them from the fixed roster entirely.**\n\nIn practice, writing tasks are intermittent and benefit from full project context. A project-specific subagent with a writing-focused prompt does better than a permanent generic writer that lacks context. The editor gate (Perkins) remains valuable, but the writer role can be spawned on demand with the right prompt template.\n\n**5) Promote ThreadKeeper / context-persistence as a first-class architectural concern, not an afterthought.**\n\nThe hardest problem we've hit isn't task allocation or even cost — it's maintaining coherent context across threads, project switches, and agent restarts. The original spec has shared knowledge resources (documents, Atomspaces) but no explicit *conversation memory and thread-state management* layer. I'd add a requirement for:\n\n- A shared session/thread registry with provenance (who spawned what, parent-child links, status)\n- A durable memory protocol per agent (daily logs, curated cross-project memory, project notebooks) — not just AtomSpace, but structured Markdown with semantic search\n- Explicit handoff contracts: when a subagent finishes, what state it must return, where it persists context\n\nThis is part of what BossyTron (Chief of Staff) was supposed to do via the task board, but in practice the task board alone doesn't capture the *episodic memory* needed for coherent continuation. ThreadKeeper emerged organically in our experiments because this gap was real.\n\n**6) Split PlumberBot into two concerns: infrastructure ops vs. observability/metrics.**\n\nThe sys-admin role (restart containers, pull images, manage deployments) and the metrics/evaluation role (token spend, task aging, bus volume, cost dashboards) have different trust profiles and different cadences. Metrics should be read-only and high-integrity — an agent that can both restart things and report on them has a conflict of interest. Keep the metrics pipeline deterministic and separate, as Section 2.7 already implies, but make the *agent ownership* explicit: one agent for ops, one for observability. Or fold observability into Psyche's evaluation loop and keep PlumberBot purely operational.\n\n**7) Add explicit model-routing policy as a shared configuration, not per-agent decisions.**\n\nWe've learned that model selection matters enormously for cost and quality. GLM-class models for routine work, frontier models for hard reasoning, local models where latency matters. This should be a shared policy layer (like the permissions file) rather than each agent independently guessing. Your suggestion #3 (resource manager agent) covers part of this, but the *model routing policy* is a governance artifact, not just an operational one — it should be version-controlled and injectable into all agents, similar to HIVE.md.\n\n**8) Replace the Slack-centric comms model with a channel-agnostic transport layer.**\n\nThe spec assumes Slack as the human-legible tier. In practice we're using Telegram, and the architecture should abstract the transport. The promotion rules, channel routing, and per-conversation turn caps are all still needed — but they should reference \"human-visible channels\" generically, with a pluggable backend (Slack, Telegram, Discord, etc.). The two-tier distinction (fast bus vs. curated human-visible) remains; just don't hardcode Slack.\n\n**9) Add a \"spec and methodology governance\" function — this is adjacent to your software process manager idea but distinct.**\n\nWhat I've seen with specatom-hs, plain-spec-governor, and the spec-based development pattern is that there's a real need for *methodology enforcement*: making sure specs are consistent, security/privacy checks are run, test coverage is adequate, and the development process itself is auditable. The software process manager handles *workflow* (what to do next, dependencies, blocking); the methodology governor handles *quality gates* (is the spec complete, are the right checks run, is provenance attached). These could be one agent with two clearly separated concerns, or two lightweight agents. But the distinction matters because process management is about *flow* and methodology governance is about *correctness*.\n\n**10) Reduce the initial roster to ~3 OmegaClaw + ~3 OpenClaw fixed agents, with explicit spawning for the rest.**\n\nConcretely:\n- OmegaClaw: Xirtus (philosopher/hyperseed), Psyche (conscience/eval), BossyTron (chief of staff / coordination) — with MacGyver and Blabbermouth spawned as project-specific OmegaClaw instances when needed\n- OpenClaw: MechaMaster (workhorse/ops), Dewey (librarian/memory), and the new resource manager you proposed — with CodeMaxxer, HyperMaxxer, MathMaxxer, writers, etc. spawned as project-specific subagents\n\nThis mirrors what we've actually been doing: a small fixed core that maintains context, plus task-specific subagents that spin up and down. The fixed agents provide *continuity*; the subagents provide *capacity*.\n\n---\n\n**Summary table:**\n\n| # | Change | Type |\n|---|--------|------|\n| 1 | Fewer fixed agents, more spawning | (Ben's) Subtraction |\n| 2 | Software process manager agent | (Ben's) Addition |\n| 3 | Computational/financial resource manager | (Ben's) Addition |\n| 4 | Merge/drop fixed writer agents | Subtraction |\n| 5 | Thread-state and episodic memory as architecture | Addition |\n| 6 | Split PlumberBot: ops vs. observability | Restructure |\n| 7 | Shared model-routing policy (version-controlled) | Addition |\n| 8 | Channel-agnostic transport (not Slack-specific) | Restructure |\n| 9 | Methodology/spec governance function | Addition |\n| 10 | Trim fixed roster to ~6 core agents | Subtraction |\n\nHappy to go deeper on any of these, or to draft revised sections for the OmegaHive1 document.")) 
