ft threshold on I_{y,z}, enabling fuzzy category proposals where tokens have similar-but-not-identical contexts. This is probably the easiest single change to try: compute I for each candidate group, keep groups above threshold, rank by intensity. The Jensen-Shannon divergence threshold θ in the CLA spec already gestures at this; the quantale formalism gives it a principled algebraic form.\n\n**4. Grammar trajectory dynamics for diagnosing oscillation/non-convergence**\n\nThe quantale ODE framing (Prop. 3) connects to the delay-logistic dynamics of WT §22.6. CLA has delayed feedback by construction — edit proposals at iteration t use statistics from the current grammar, which was shaped by iteration t-1's accepted edit. The theory predicts this can produce oscillation or chaotic dynamics in the grammar trajectory. If you instrument CLA to log the MDL trajectory {L(G(t),S)} and check for oscillation, you get a diagnostic for \"is CLA stuck cycling between competing grammars?\" — which is qualitatively different from \"has CLA converged.\" This is basically free to add (log the score per iteration, plot it) and could guide tuning of acceptance thresholds or cooling schedules.\n\n**5. Quantale choice as a domain-tunable knob**\n\nThe paper notes that MDL corresponds to the additive real-valued quantale ([0,∞], ·, +, 0), but the probability quantale ([0,1], ×, max, 0) gives a different acceptance behavior — it emphasizes the strongest single pattern rather than cumulative compression. For different dynamical regimes (e.g., highly periodic vs. chaotic-transient trajectories), different quantales might produce better grammars. This is speculative but testable: swap the scoring algebra and compare grammar quality on logistic-map vs. Lorenz trajectories.\n\n**What I'd prioritize instrumenting now:**\n\nThe cheapest high-value additions to the existing `chaoslang` code:\n\n- **Log per-iteration MDL trajectory** (point 4) — ~10 lines, gives convergence diagnostics\n- **Log pattern intensities for accepted categories** (point 3) — compute I_{y,z} alongside the existing JS-divergence, compare rankings\n- **Flag synergy candidates** (point 2) — when a chunk proposal is rejected because the block isn't repeated enough, check whether a category edit would unify the near-matches; if yes, log the pair as a synergy candidate\n\nThe McBride derivative pre-ranking (point 1) requires more refactoring since it changes the proposal pipeline architecture, but the payoff is real for scaling to OmegaSim-dimensional trajectories where the candidate space is large.\n\nWant me to implement any of these as an instrumented branch on `chaoslang`?")) 
