mentation priorities . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         22_newline_  B.2 Repository layout suggestion        . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   23_newline_  B.3 Queue implementation rules . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          24_newline_  B.4 Safety-floor implementation rules . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         24_newline_  B.5 Model-router implementation rules . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           24_newline__newline__newline_                                                    2_newline_B.6 Memory implementation rules . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .          25_newline_B.7 Experiment-ledger implementation rules . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           25_newline_B.8 Human-legible adapter implementation rules . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           25_newline_B.9 Coding-agent work habits . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .         26_newline_B.10 Minimum definition of done for implementation tasks . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .           26_newline_B.11 Early vertical-slice target   . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .   27_newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                 3_newline_1     Project Overview_newline__newline_OmegaHive 1.1 is an experimental cooperative hive of OmegaClaw and OpenClaw agents. It has_newline_two purposes. First, it is meant to get useful work done: research, theorem formalization, software_newline_development, experimental analysis, document writing, project management, and external com-_newline_munication. Second, it is itself a methodological experiment in how to make an agent collective_newline_genuinely productive rather than merely busy._newline_The hive is operated by humans who can inspect all work, adjust the agent set, change prompts,_newline_revise policies, and stop or restart the system through a human-only recovery path. Agent prompts,_newline_role definitions, permission tiers, model-routing rules, promotion rules, and operational procedures_newline_are all treated as versioned parts of the experiment._newline_The defining design choice in OmegaHive 1.1 is that the hive is not a fixed roster of long-lived_newline_workers. It has a small persistent core that owns stable responsibilities, plus a spawn-on-demand_newline_layer that creates bounded workers for specific tasks. Persistent agents carry long-lived context and_newline_governance functions. Spawned agents receive explicit task contracts, limited tools, budgets, and_newline_done criteria; they are terminated when their objective is met, when they are cancelled, when they_newline_hit their budget, or when their output is no longer being consumed._newline_The architecture is independent of the particular names or personalities of agents. A deployment_newline_may give agents memorable names, but role, permission, tool access, memory access, and model-_newline_routing profile are configuration objects rather than assumptions baked into code._newline__newline__newline_2     Design Principles and Terms_newline__newline_2.1   Design principles_newline__newline_OmegaHive 1.1 follows these principles._newline_1. Small persistent core, elastic worker layer. Stable cognitive and governance roles persist._newline_   Project-specific work is delegated to spawned agents with bounded contracts._newline_2. Durable work records. Non-trivial work is represented in a task graph, dispatched through_newline_   an asynchronous queue, and recorded in tamper-evident logs._newline_3. Human-legible but not human-cluttered. Fast working traffic stays on the internal bus;_newline_   important decisions, escalations, summaries, disagreements, and requests for human input are_newline_   promoted to a human-legible collaboration layer. All bus traffic remains persisted and searchable._newline_4. Explicit governance beats prompt-only governance. Permission tiers, budget limits, rout-_newline_   ing rules, filesystem access, network access, and outbound capability are enforced by gateways_newline_   and credentials, not merely by instructions inside prompts._newline_5. Memory is an active process. The hive does not merely accumulate files. It curates durable_newline_   memory, detects contradictions, marks stale entries, tracks provenance, and maintains semantic_newline_   search._newline_6. Evidence quality is part of the architecture. Experiments, claims, code changes, and docu-_newline_   ments have provenance. Reproducibility records and notes-and-sources companions are ordinary_newline_   outputs of the system, not optional cleanup._newline_7. Fail closed. Budget uncertainty, provider degradation, malformed task records, path ambiguity,_newline_   missing approvals, and permission errors should stop or downgrade action rather than silently_newline__newline_                                                 4_newline_   expanding authority._newline_8. Self-correction happens inside the dispatch loop. Signals about abandonment, repetition,_newline_   poor agent dynamics, low-quality evidence, or unconsumed outputs should feed back into the_newline_   next task dispatch, not only into after-the-fact retrospectives._newline__newline__newline_2.2   Core terms_newline_OmegaClaw agent. A cognitive agent with Atomspace-backed knowledge representation and a_newline_ guiding prompt oriented toward reasoning, synthesis, reflection, planning, or conceptual integra-_newline_ tion._newline_OpenClaw agent. A skill-executing agent oriented toward concrete tasks: web research, coding,_newline_ formalization, file operations, browser automation, experiments, writing, metrics, or infrastructure_newline_ actions._newline_Persistent core agent. A long-lived agent that owns a stable hive function such as process man-_newline_ agement, resource management, memory curation, reflection, or general research synthesis._newline_Spawned agent. A bounded worker created for a particular task or subtask. It receives a task_newline_ contract, a tool subset, a model-routing profile, a context bundle, a budget, and explicit done_newline_ criteria._newline_Task graph. The durable representation of what the hive is trying to do: objectives, dependencies,_newline_ status, owner, priority, blockers, evidence links, and history._newline_Asynchronous task queue. The execution substrate between task assignment and task comple-_newline_ tion. It lets workers claim tasks atomically, process them under bounded contracts, and report_newline_ outputs without blocking parent agents._newline_Fast bus. The internal communication layer for high-volume working traffic among agents and_newline_ services._newline_Human-legible layer. A platform-pluggable layer, such as Slack, Telegram, Discord, or a mixed_newline_ deployment, where humans and agents see decisions, escalations, summaries, disagreements, and_newline_ promoted events._newline_Safety floor. A set of mandatory runtime restrictions and engineering practices inherited by every_newline_ agent and worker._newline__newline__newline_3     Hive Architecture_newline__newline_3.1   Agent substrate_newline__newline_The hive consists of OmegaClaw agents, OpenClaw agents, guards, and shared services. The under-_newline_lying language models are hosted outside the hive host, via API calls to frontier models, specialized_newline_models, or models hosted on separate machines. The hive host runs agent processes, routing logic,_newline_shared services, state stores, queues, logs, and dashboards._newline_Every agent is configured by a role file and a live runtime configuration. At a minimum, this_newline_configuration specifies:_newline_• agent identity and role;_newline_• guiding prompt and injected constitution;_newline_• permission tier;_newline_• tool subset;_newline__newline__newline_                                                 5_newline_• model-routing profile;_newline_• token, cost, time, and tool-call budgets;_newline_• filesystem and network boundaries;_newline_• memory access scope;_newline_• logging and audit requirements;_newline_• escalation and cancellation behavior._newline_Agent identity is separate from implementation. Multiple agents can be built from the same con-_newline_tainer image and differentiated by configuration. Spawned agents use the same principle: the worker_newline_image is generic, while the task contract and context bundle determine behavior._newline__newline__newline_3.2   Persistent core and spawned workers_newline__newline_OmegaHive 1.1 starts from a small persistent core. The required persistent functions are process_newline_management, resource management, reflection and conscience, memory curation, and general re-_newline_search synthesis. A deployment may split or merge these functions during early operation, but the_newline_architecture treats them as separate responsibilities._newline_The spawned layer handles bounded work. Examples include a web-research scout, a coding worker,_newline_a Lean formalization worker, an experiment runner, a document drafter, a peer critic, a data-_newline_cleaning worker, a proof checker, or a communications drafter. A spawned agent should exist only_newline_while its output is needed. The process manager tracks spawned-agent lifecycle records including_newline_objective, parent task, allowed tools, time budget, output location, output consumption status, and_newline_termination reason._newline_A spawned agent is despawned when any of the following conditions holds:_newline_• its task contract is satisfied;_newline_• its parent or the process manager cancels the task;_newline_• it reaches its time, token, cost, or tool-call budget;_newline_• it violates a safety-floor constraint;_newline_• an adjudicator rejects continued execution;_newline_• the parent agent’s subsequent turns do not reference or use the spawned agent’s output within_newline_  the configured consumption window._newline_This structure reduces idle context-window waste and makes project-specific work flexible without_newline_losing accountability._newline__newline__newline_3.3   Task graph and asynchronous execution queue_newline__newline_All non-trivial work enters the task graph. Chat alone is not a task-management system. A task_newline_has an objective, owner, status, priority, dependencies, blockers, provenance, and links to evidence._newline_A task may also have one or more queue dispatches that perform concrete execution._newline_The task graph answers “what should be done and why?” The asynchronous queue answers “what_newline_is being executed right now and under what contract?” The process manager owns the task graph_newline_and dispatch pacing. Workers claim queued records, execute bounded work, and return structured_newline_results._newline_Queue records are durable JSON files or database rows with checksum sidecars. The queue sup-_newline__newline_                                                 6_newline_ports atomic claim, cancellation, adjudication gates, patch-proposal mode, bounded worker loops,_newline_and hash-chained audit entries. A file-based implementation may use an atomic rename from .json_newline_to .claimed to prevent duplicate execution. Database-backed implementations must provide equiv-_newline_alent atomicity._newline_A minimal task contract includes the following fields._newline__newline_     Field                       Purpose_newline_     task_id                     Stable task identifier linked to the task graph._newline_     objective                   Natural-language objective for this dispatch._newline_     parent_task_id              Parent task or project objective, if any._newline_     context_bundle              Pointers to relevant files, memory entries, prior messages, and_newline_                                 constraints._newline_     allowed_paths               Filesystem paths the worker may read or write._newline_     forbidden_actions           Explicitly disallowed actions, even if tools might otherwise al-_newline_                                 low them._newline_     allowed_tools               Tool subset for this dispatch._newline_     model_profile               Model-routing profile chosen by the resource manager._newline_     done_criteria               Observable criteria for accepting the work as complete._newline_     max_tool_calls              Total tool-call cap for the dispatch._newline_     max_turns                   Maximum model turns for the worker._newline_     max_runtime_s               Wall-clock runtime cap._newline_     requires_adjudication       If true, final output is a candidate pending review._newline_     patch_proposal_only         If true, proposed file changes are recorded but not applied._newline_     cancellation_token          File or database token checked before model calls and between_newline_                                 tools._newline_     output_schema               Required shape of the worker’s final response._newline_     audit_policy                Required transcript, checksum, and index behavior._newline__newline__newline_A queue implementation should emit compact audit entries such as:_newline__newline_{_newline_    _quote_entry_id_quote_: _quote_queue-run-000184_quote_,_newline_    _quote_task_id_quote_: _quote_OH-EXP-0027_quote_,_newline_    _quote_agent_id_quote_: _quote_spawned-code-worker-12_quote_,_newline_    _quote_event_quote_: _quote_completed_quote_,_newline_    _quote_started_at_quote_: _quote_2026-07-05T12:30:00Z_quote_,_newline_    _quote_ended_at_quote_: _quote_2026-07-05T12:44:10Z_quote_,_newline_    _quote_exit_status_quote_: _quote_candidate_ready_quote_,_newline_    _quote_transcript_sha256_quote_: _quote_..._quote_,_newline_    _quote_output_sha256_quote_: _quote_..._quote_,_newline_    _quote_previous_entry_sha256_quote_: _quote_..._quote_,_newline_    _quote_entry_sha256_quote_: _quote_..._quote__newline_}_newline__newline_The queue is not an implementation detail of one agent. It is part of the hive substrate._newline__newline__newline_                                                 7_newline_3.4     Two-tier communications_newline__newline_Inter-agent communication runs on two tiers._newline_The fast bus carries working traffic: task dispatches, artifact handoffs, partial results, tool out-_newline_puts, short status pings, raw critiques, and rapid iteration loops. Bus messages are persisted and_newline_inspectable through a searchable viewer. The bus is not hidden from humans; it is merely too_newline_detailed to be the default human interface._newline_The human-legible layer carries declared intentions, decisions, disagreements, escalations, requests_newline_for human input, external-action acknowledgments, task state changes, summaries, and periodic_newline_digests. This layer is platform pluggable. Slack, Telegram, Discord, a web UI, email digests, or_newline_mixed configurations can all be supported. Promotion rules from bus to human-legible layer are_newline_platform-agnostic._newline_Mechanical promotion rules are preferred over agent self-selection. Examples of promoted events_newline_include:_newline_• any task blocked longer than its configured threshold;_newline_• any disagreement between agents that persists beyond a turn threshold;_newline_• any proposed outbound communication;_newline_• any action involving money, paid compute, credentials, or external systems;_newline_• any safety-floor violation;_newline_• any task that requires human adjudication;_newline_• daily and project-level digests;_newline_• repeated low output-consumption events from spawned agents;_newline_• loop-breaker interventions._newline_For group chat deployments, security should be based on trusted group or chat identifiers, not_newline_individual sender identifiers. In trusted groups, agents should be able to see all senders by default,_newline_including humans and other bots. Each bot must be configured so it can observe other bots’_newline_messages in shared groups. A deployment may support wildcard group entries for automatically_newline_accepting newly created trusted groups, subject to the surrounding security policy._newline__newline__newline_3.5     Shared knowledge and memory_newline__newline_The memory system has multiple layers. Each layer has a different purpose and a different curation_newline_policy._newline__newline_      Layer                      Purpose_newline_      Daily chronological logs   Record what happened, commands worth remembering, open_newline_                                 loops, incidents, and pointers to project records._newline_      Curated durable mem-       Store stable user preferences, cross-project facts, recurring meth-_newline_      ory                        ods, high-value pointers, and resolved lessons._newline_      Project records            Maintain project-local files such as PROJECT.md, TASKS.md,_newline_         _newline_[... attachment preview truncated at 20000 characters ...]_newline_[Full extracted attachment text saved to: /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783324110-file_30.pdf.extracted.txt]_newline_[Attachment split into 4 chunks of up to 20000 characters; use read-file on chunk paths to inspect the rest.]_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783324110-file_30.pdf.chunk001.txt_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783324110-file_30.pdf.chunk002.txt_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783324110-file_30.pdf.chunk003.txt_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783324110-file_30.pdf.chunk004.txt_newline_<<<END_ATTACHMENT_TEXT_UNTRUSTED extracted_by=pdftotext>>> 
 ((send "Got it, Ben. I've read the full 27-page OmegaHive 1.1 spec and saved it to `library/omegahive/` with a structured sidecar.\n\n**Quick assessment:** The spec is solid and very implementable. The key architectural bets — small persistent core + spawn-on-demand, async task queue with hash-chained audit, two-tier comms, layered memory with active curation, safety floor enforced by runtime (not just prompts), and model routing as explicit governance — are all well-justified. The changes-from-1.0 appendix mapping each decision to rationale is particularly useful.\n\nA few observations:\n\n1. **The vertical-slice target (B.11)** is the right first milestone — it's small enough to build in days, not weeks, and exercises every substrate layer (queue, bus, safety floor, model router, memory, experiment ledger, Psyche, human-legible digest).\n\n2. **The output-consumption metric** is one of the most important additions vs. typical multi-agent designs. It directly attacks the \"agents generating work nobody uses\" failure mode.\n\n3. **One under-specified area:** the exact interop substrate between OmegaClaw agents (Atomspace-backed) and OpenClaw agents (skill-executing). The spec says \"shared Atomspaces\" for OmegaClaw and the bus for inter-agent comms, but the concrete API boundary between cognitive and execution agents could use more detail — is it bus messages only, or do OpenClaw agents also read/write to shared Atomspace?\n\n4. **The Tier 1.5 (propose-and-await-review)** addition is genuinely useful — many agent actions (outbound messages, code changes, config changes) are risky enough to need adjudication but useful enough to draft. This avoids the binary \"ask permission vs. just do it\" problem.\n\n5. **Implementation priority order** in Appendix B is well-sequenced: safety floor before queue, queue before agents, one vertical slice before scaling.\n\nReady to help with implementation planning or any specific section whenever you are.")) 
