newline_ 2:    match kind(expr) with_newline_ 3:      Choice(alts) →_newline_ 4:        return split_choice_bounded(alts, stats, budget)_newline_ 5:      Match(scrut, _) if not is_value(scrut) →_newline_ 6:        return split_match_symbolic(scrut, stats, budget)_newline_ 7:      Prim(_quote_kb_query_quote_, [pattern]) →_newline_ 8:        return split_kb_symbolic(pattern, stats, budget)_newline_ 9:      _ → [expr]_newline_10: end function_newline_11: function SplitChoiceBounded(alts, stats, budget)_newline_12:                                                              . Estimate branch probabilities_newline_13:    probs ← map(λalt. estimate_probability(alt.guard, stats), alts)_newline_14:    sorted ← sort_by_probability(zip(alts, probs))_newline_15:                                                    . Take most likely branches up to budget_newline_16:    selected ← []_newline_17:    cumulative_prob ← 0_newline_18:    for (alt, prob) in sorted do_newline_19:        if len(selected) < budget and cumulative_prob < 0.95 then_newline_20:            selected.append(alt)_newline_21:            cumulative_prob += prob_newline_22:        else_newline_23:            break_newline_24:        end if_newline_25:    end for_newline_26:                                                                . Add catch-all for soundness_newline_27:    if cumulative_prob < 1.0 then_newline_28:        selected.append(make_catch_all(alts))_newline_29:    end if_newline_30:    return selected_newline_31: end function_newline__newline__newline_6.3     Canonical Keys and Termination_newline_6.3.1    Canonical Key Encoding_newline_Keys are canonically encoded to prevent explosion:_newline__newline_Structure CanonicalPathSig {_newline_  head: Symbol                              // Top-level symbol_newline_  shape: CompactShape                       // Depth-3 arity vector_newline_  tags: SortedMultiset<Symbol>              // Constructor tags_newline_  depth: Int_newline_  kb_sig: CanonicalKBSig                    // See below_newline_  effect_sig: CanonicalEffectSig            // See below_newline_}_newline__newline__newline__newline_                                               9_newline_Structure CanonicalKBSig {_newline_  predicates: SortedList<(Predicate, FixedArgMask)>_newline_  access_pattern: BitVector // Which positions are accessed_newline_}_newline__newline_Structure CanonicalEffectSig {_newline_  effects: SortedMultiset<EffectClass>_newline_  resources: SortedSet<ResourceID>_newline_}_newline__newline_6.3.2    Subsumption Relation_newline_Formal subsumption for sound folding:_newline__newline_Algorithm 10 KeySubsumption_newline_ 1: function Subsumes(key1, key2)_newline_ 2:                                                                      . Structural subsumption_newline_ 3:    if key1.head 6= key2.head then return false_newline_ 4:    end if_newline_ 5:    if not shape_subsumes(key1.shape, key2.shape) then return false_newline_ 6:    end if_newline_ 7:                                                  . KB subsumption: key1 must be more general_newline_ 8:    for (pred2, mask2) in key2.kb_sig.predicates do_newline_ 9:        found ← false_newline_10:        for (pred1, mask1) in key1.kb_sig.predicates do_newline_11:            if pred1 = pred2 and mask1 ⊆ mask2 then_newline_12:                found ← true_newline_13:                break_newline_14:            end if_newline_15:        end for_newline_16:        if not found then return false_newline_17:        end if_newline_18:    end for_newline_19:                                              . Effect subsumption: key1 must have fewer effects_newline_20:    if not key1.effect_sig.effects ⊆ key2.effect_sig.effects then_newline_21:        return false_newline_22:    end if_newline_23:    return true_newline_24: end function_newline__newline__newline__newline_7     Knowledge Base Specialization_newline_7.1     Incremental Saturation_newline_Use semi-naive evaluation for incremental updates:_newline__newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                10_newline_Algorithm 11 IncrementalSaturation_newline_ 1: function SaturateIncremental(kb, new_facts, indices)_newline_ 2:    delta_old ← new_facts_newline_ 3:    all_derived ← kb.facts ∪ new_facts_newline_ 4:    while delta_old 6= {} do_newline_ 5:        delta_new ← {}_newline_ 6:        for rule in kb.rules do_newline_ 7:                                                 . At least one premise must be from delta_old_newline_ 8:            bindings ← match_with_delta(rule.body, all_derived, delta_old, indices)_newline_ 9:            for binding in bindings do_newline_10:               derived ← instantiate(rule.head, binding)_newline_11:               if derived ∈/ all_derived then_newline_12:                   delta_new ← delta_new ∪ {derived}_newline_13:                   update_indices(indices, derived)_newline_14:                   record_provenance(derived, rule, binding)_newline_15:               end if_newline_16:            end for_newline_17:        end for_newline_18:        all_derived ← all_derived ∪ delta_new_newline_19:        delta_old ← delta_new_newline_20:    end while_newline_21:    return all_derived_newline_22: end function_newline__newline__newline_7.2   Versioned Indices_newline_Indices are versioned for consistency:_newline__newline_Structure VersionedIndex {_newline_  version: Int_newline_  index: PersistentHashMap<Key, Set<FactID>>_newline_  delta_since: Map<Version, Set<FactID>>_newline_  statistics: IndexStatistics_newline_}_newline__newline_Function query_index(index, pattern, min_version):_newline_  if index.version >= min_version:_newline_    return index.lookup(pattern)_newline_  else:_newline_    // Index is stale, need replan_newline_    return null_newline__newline__newline_8     Evolutionary Algorithm Specialization_newline_8.1   Gated Specialization_newline_Only specialize when amortization time is sufficient:_newline__newline__newline__newline_                                                11_newline_Algorithm 12 GatedEvolutionarySpecialization_newline_ 1: function ShouldSpecializeFitness(profile, config)_newline_ 2:    eval_cost ← profile.avg_fitness_eval_time_newline_ 3:    population ← config.population_size_newline_ 4:    generations ← config.expected_generations_newline_ 5:    total_evals ← population * generations_newline_ 6:    specialization_cost ← estimate_specialization_cost(profile)_newline_ 7:    amortization_time ← specialization_cost / eval_cost_newline_ 8:  _newline_[... attachment preview truncated at 20000 characters ...]_newline_[Full extracted attachment text saved to: /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783324220-file_32.pdf.extracted.txt]_newline_[Attachment split into 2 chunks of up to 20000 characters; use read-file on chunk paths to inspect the rest.]_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783324220-file_32.pdf.chunk001.txt_newline_- /home/openclaw/tmp/omegaclaw-telegram-attachments/1783324220-file_32.pdf.chunk002.txt_newline_<<<END_ATTACHMENT_TEXT_UNTRUSTED extracted_by=pdftotext>>> 
 ((send "Good — now I've read both documents in full. This October 2025 MORK-native supercompiler design is clearly the predecessor that the CeTTa document evolves from. Here's how they relate:\n\n**What carried forward to CeTTa:**\n\n1. **Minimal core IR philosophy** — M-Core (§3) maps directly to CeTTa's residual MeTTa + operator schema approach. The node types (Sym, Var, Con, App, Match, Choice, Prim) are the same substrate, just rebranded.\n\n2. **Effect algebra** — The commutativity relations (Algorithm 1) and sink-free semantics (§4.3) are the direct ancestor of CeTTa's effect system (§2.4 in the CeTTa doc). The key insight — that monotonic multiset additions enable permanent caching, speculation without rollback, and append-only indices — survives intact.\n\n3. **Canonical keys for termination** — The CanonicalPathSig / CanonicalKBSig / CanonicalEffectSig structures (§6.3) are the precursor to CeTTa's CettaPack manifest guards. The subsumption relation (Algorithm 10) is the same idea: fold when a more general key subsumes a specific one.\n\n4. **Bounded splitting with statistics** — Algorithm 9's probability-guided branch selection with a 0.95 cumulative probability cap and catch-all for soundness is essentially what CeTTa's Pattern 2 (local search episode) needs.\n\n5. **Bisimulation for MM2 compilation correctness** — §9's proof structure is retained in CeTTa's certificate layer (§A.5).\n\n**What CeTTa changes or supersedes:**\n\n1. **Multi-geometry IR is new.** This document commits to a single minimal core IR (M-Core) with domain-specific compilation to primitives. CeTTa's key innovation — *deferred presentation choice* among C, PathMap/trie, factor graph, tensor, MM2, and residual MeTTa — is absent here. This document's architecture is \"compile everything to M-Core then supercompile.\" CeTTa says \"don't commit to a presentation until you've profiled.\"\n\n2. **No CettaPack / guarded artifact system.** This document has versioned indices (§7.2) but nothing like the CettaPack manifest with runtime guard checking and automatic fallback. The safety architecture is weaker — correctness depends on the supercompiler doing the right thing, rather than on the loader verifying guards at execution time.\n\n3. **No PathMap / native rematch distinction.** This document treats kb_query as a monolithic primitive. CeTTa's two-stage strategy (PathMap narrows candidates, native seeded rematch decides) is a refinement that doesn't appear here.\n\n4. **Query planner is the primary optimization layer here.** In CeTTa, the query planner role is absorbed into the broader \"planner chooses presentation\" step. The cardinality estimation and effect-aware join ordering algorithms (Algorithms 2-6) are still relevant but become inputs to presentation choice rather than the optimization itself.\n\n5. **No mention of Lean/LeaTTa certificates.** §12 claims formal correctness properties but through traditional proof structures (bisimulation, induction). CeTTa's plan to emit machine-checkable certificates and have Lean verify them is a significant upgrade in trustworthiness.\n\n**What this document has that CeTTa could use:**\n\n1. **The full effect algebra and commutativity algorithm** — CeTTa references an effect system but doesn't specify it. This document's Algorithm 1 is concrete and implementable. It should be pulled into CeTTa as the effect taxonomy spec I flagged as missing.\n\n2. **Incremental statistics under monotonic growth** (§5.2) — The delta statistics maintenance and adaptive replanning algorithms are directly relevant to CeTTa's hot-region detection (which I flagged as unspecified). The growth-rate tracking and drift-based replanning thresholds are a concrete answer to \"how do we know when to recompile.\"\n\n3. **Evolutionary specialization gating** (§8) — The amortization analysis for fitness function specialization is a useful pattern that CeTTa's Pattern 4 (factor kernels) could adopt for deciding when specialization pays off.\n\n4. **Semi-naive KB saturation** (Algorithm 11) — Concrete incremental saturation with delta tracking. Relevant if CeTTa's Pattern 3 (active subgraph tensor extraction) needs to maintain consistent views of a changing space.\n\n**Overall assessment:** The CeTTa document is the right evolutionary step — it generalizes from \"compile to one IR and supercompile\" to \"choose the right presentation and guard it.\" But this document fills several of the gaps I flagged in CeTTa: the effect algebra, the statistics machinery, and the incremental maintenance story. The two should be treated as companion documents, with CeTTa as the architecture spec and this as the algorithms-and-formal-semantics reference.\n\nThe implementation roadmap here (§10) is also more concrete than CeTTa's MVP plan — Phase 0 through Phase 4 with clear deliverables. CeTTa's MVP (one match site → C kernel) is a subset of this Phase 2.")) 
