 ((send "Yes — I have a few additions to the CeTTa thread that go beyond what's in the feedback summary document. Let me add them.\n\nA few things I'd add to the CeTTa/supercompilation discussion, beyond what's already in the compiled feedback summary:\n\n**1. Cross-space kernel guards.** The manifest model handles single-space revision checks, but MeTTa programs routinely query *multiple* spaces in a single match. A CettaPack for a cross-space join needs per-space revision guards — if any constituent space has drifted, the kernel must fall back. This is mechanically straightforward (just check all manifest space-revision fields) but it's not specified, and the performance characteristics of multi-space guard checking on the hot path matter for real MeTTa programs.\n\n**2. Supercompilation vs. meta-rules.** MeTTa's distinctive power is meta-level rules — rules that rewrite rules. The CeTTa document's optimization patterns all assume the hot match site is *stable*. But if a meta-rule rewrite changes the structure of the match site itself, the CettaPack is invalidated in a way that a space-revision bump won't catch. The manifest should carry a structural hash of the *match pattern* (not just the space), so meta-rule-driven structural changes trigger fallback. This is the trickiest correctness issue I see — it's not just a spec gap, it's a semantic subtlety about what \"the same kernel\" means across program evolution.\n\n**3. Incremental recompilation, not just binary fallback.** The guard model is binary: guard passes → use kernel; guard fails → generic evaluation. But for append-only spaces that grow gradually, you don't want to fall back to generic eval for *everything* when the space grows by 5%. The v2 document's semi-naive saturation (Algorithm 11) and incremental statistics (Section 5.2) suggest an intermediate path: *incrementally update* the kernel's internal state (e.g., PathMap index entries) rather than falling all the way back. This would make the guard m